pre", "resource-development", "t1608.007"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1608.007 **Tactics:** resource-development **Platforms:** PRE **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1608/007 Adversaries may manipulate publicly accessible web content to influence generative AI systems or AI-powered search engines into retrieving, citing, or recommendi"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1696-001", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1696.001 Automate CAPTCHA Completion", "tags": ["automate captcha completion", "saas", "stealth", "t1696.001"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1696.001 **Tactics:** stealth **Platforms:** SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1696/001 Adversaries may attempt to use bots to successfully solve CAPTCHA challenges. CAPTCHA challenges are typically used by defenders to detect and prevent bot and spam traffic f"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1696-002", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1696.002 Avoid CAPTCHA", "tags": ["avoid captcha", "saas", "stealth", "t1696.002"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1696.002 **Tactics:** stealth **Platforms:** SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1696/002 Adversaries may avoid CAPTCHA challenges by mimicking human behavior. Automation tests, such as CAPTCHA, are typically used by defenders to detect and prevent bot and spam t"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1685-007", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1685.007 Impair Autonomous Defenses", "tags": ["defense-impairment", "impair autonomous defenses", "saas", "t1685.007", "windows"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1685.007 **Tactics:** defense-impairment **Platforms:** Windows, SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1685/007 Adversaries may use methods like [Indirect Prompt Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1698) to disrupt, disable, or otherwise impair autonomo"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1700", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1700 AI-Enabled Exfiltration", "tags": ["ai-enabled exfiltration", "exfiltration", "saas", "t1700", "windows"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1700 **Tactics:** exfiltration **Platforms:** Windows, SaaS **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1700 Adversaries may utilize [Direct Prompt Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1699), [Indirect Prompt Injection](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1698) or"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-credential-access", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Credential Access", "tags": ["credential access", "credential-access", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to steal account names, passwords, or other secrets that enable access to resources. Credential access represents techniques that can be used by adversaries to obtain access to or control over passwords, tokens, cryptographic keys, or other values that could be used by an adv"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-defense-impairment", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Defense Impairment", "tags": ["defense impairment", "defense-impairment", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to break security mechanisms, pipelines, and tooling so defenders can’t see or trust what’s happening. Defense Impairment consists of techniques that degrade, disable, or undermine the effectiveness and trustworthiness of security controls and monitoring mechanisms. These tec"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-execution", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Execution", "tags": ["execution", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to run code or manipulate system functions, parameters, and data in an unauthorized way. Execution consists of techniques that result in adversary-controlled code running on a local or remote system, device, or other asset. This execution may also rely on unknowing end users "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-impact", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Impact", "tags": ["impact", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to manipulate, interrupt, or destroy your ICS systems, data, and their surrounding environment. Impact consists of techniques that adversaries use to disrupt, compromise, destroy, and manipulate the integrity and availability of control system operations, processes, devices, "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-persistence", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Persistence", "tags": ["persistence", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to maintain their foothold in your ICS environment. Persistence consists of techniques that adversaries use to maintain access to ICS systems and devices across restarts, changed credentials, and other interruptions that could cut off their access. Techniques used for persist"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-privilege-escalation", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Privilege Escalation", "tags": ["privilege escalation", "privilege-escalation", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to gain higher-level permissions. Privilege Escalation consists of techniques that adversaries use to gain higher-level permissions on a system or network. Adversaries can often enter and explore a network with unprivileged access but require elevated permissions to follow th"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-lateral-movement", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Lateral Movement", "tags": ["lateral movement", "lateral-movement", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to move through your ICS environment. Lateral Movement consists of techniques that adversaries use to enter and control remote systems on a network. These techniques abuse default credentials, known accounts, and vulnerable services, and may also leverage dual-homed devices a"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-stealth", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Stealth", "tags": ["stealth", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to hide and conceal their actions, appearing as normal behavior. Stealth consists of techniques that reduce the likelihood of detection by blending in with legitimate activity or minimizing observable signals. These techniques are characterized by concealment behaviors, such "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-exfiltration", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Exfiltration", "tags": ["exfiltration", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to steal data. Exfiltration refers to techniques and attributes that result or aid in the adversary removing files and information from the targeted mobile device. In the mobile environment, mobile devices are frequently connected to networks outside enterprise control such a"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-discovery", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Discovery", "tags": ["discovery", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is locating information to assess and identify their targets in your environment. Discovery consists of techniques that adversaries use to survey your ICS environment and gain knowledge about the internal network, control system devices, and how their processes interact. These techniqu"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-collection", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Collection", "tags": ["collection", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to gather data of interest and domain knowledge on your ICS environment to inform their goal. Collection consists of techniques adversaries use to gather domain knowledge and obtain contextual feedback in an ICS environment. This tactic is often performed as part of [Discover"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-resource-development", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Resource Development", "tags": ["resource development", "resource-development", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to establish resources they can use to support operations. Resource Development consists of techniques that involve adversaries creating, purchasing, or compromising/stealing resources that can be used to support targeting. Such resources include infrastructure, accounts, or "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-reconnaissance", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Reconnaissance", "tags": ["reconnaissance", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to gather information they can use to plan future operations. Reconnaissance consists of techniques that involve adversaries actively or passively gathering information that can be used to support targeting. Such information may include details of the victim organization, inf"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-command-and-control", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Command and Control", "tags": ["command and control", "command-and-control", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to communicate with and control compromised systems, controllers, and platforms with access to your ICS environment. Command and Control consists of techniques that adversaries use to communicate with and send commands to compromised systems, devices, controllers, and platfor"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-tactic-initial-access", "source": "mitre", "title": "Tactic: Initial Access", "tags": ["initial access", "initial-access", "tactic"], "summary": "The adversary is trying to get into your ICS environment. Initial Access consists of techniques that adversaries may use as entry vectors to gain an initial foothold within an ICS environment. These techniques include compromising operational technology assets, IT resources in the OT network, and ex"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1174", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1174 Password Filter DLL Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "password filter dll mitigation", "t1174"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1174 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1174 Ensure only valid password filters are registered. Filter DLLs must be present in Windows installation directory (<code>C:\\Windows\\System32\\</code> by default) of a domain controller and/or local computer with a correspon"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1151", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1151 Space after Filename Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "space after filename mitigation", "t1151"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1151 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1151 Prevent files from having a trailing space after the extension."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1148", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1148 HISTCONTROL Mitigation", "tags": ["histcontrol mitigation", "mitigation", "t1148"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1148 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1148 Prevent users from changing the <code>HISTCONTROL</code> environment variable (Citation: Securing bash history). Also, make sure that the <code>HISTCONTROL</code> environment variable is set to “ignoredup” instead of “ign"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1081", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1081 Credentials in Files Mitigation", "tags": ["credentials in files mitigation", "mitigation", "t1081"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1081 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1081 Establish an organizational policy that prohibits password storage in files. Ensure that developers and system administrators are aware of the risk associated with having plaintext passwords in software configuration file"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1162", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1162 Login Item Mitigation", "tags": ["login item mitigation", "mitigation", "t1162"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1162 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1162 Restrict users from being able to create their own login items. Additionally, holding the shift key during login prevents apps from opening automatically (Citation: Re-Open windows on Mac)."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1166", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1166 Setuid and Setgid Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "setuid and setgid mitigation", "t1166"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1166 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1166 Applications with known vulnerabilities or known shell escapes should not have the setuid or setgid bits set to reduce potential damage if an application is compromised. Additionally, the number of programs with setuid or"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1223", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1223 Compiled HTML File Mitigation", "tags": ["compiled html file mitigation", "mitigation", "t1223"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1223 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1223 Consider blocking download/transfer and execution of potentially uncommon file types known to be used in adversary campaigns, such as CHM files. (Citation: PaloAlto Preventing Opportunistic Attacks Apr 2016) Also consider"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1488", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1488 Data Destruction Mitigation", "tags": ["data destruction mitigation", "mitigation", "t1488"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1488 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1488 Consider implementing IT disaster recovery plans that contain procedures for taking regular data backups that can be used to restore organizational data.(Citation: Ready.gov IT DRP) Ensure backups are stored off system an"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1084", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1084 Windows Management Instrumentation Event Subscription Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1084", "windows management instrumentation event subscription mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1084 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1084 Disabling WMI services may cause system instability and should be evaluated to assess the impact to a network. By default, only administrators are allowed to connect remotely using WMI; restrict other users that are allow"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1044", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1044 File System Permissions Weakness Mitigation", "tags": ["file system permissions weakness mitigation", "mitigation", "t1044"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1044 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1044 Use auditing tools capable of detecting file system permissions abuse opportunities on systems within an enterprise and correct them. Limit privileges of user accounts and groups so that only authorized administrators can"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1103", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1103 AppInit DLLs Mitigation", "tags": ["appinit dlls mitigation", "mitigation", "t1103"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1103 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1103 Upgrade to Windows 8 or later and enable secure boot. Identify and block potentially malicious software that may be executed through AppInit DLLs by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLocker, (Cita"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1159", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1159 Launch Agent Mitigation", "tags": ["launch agent mitigation", "mitigation", "t1159"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1159 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1159 Restrict user's abilities to create Launch Agents with group policy."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1031", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1031 Network Intrusion Prevention", "tags": ["m1031", "mitigation", "network intrusion prevention"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1031 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1031 Use intrusion detection signatures to block traffic at network boundaries."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1117", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1117 Regsvr32 Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "regsvr32 mitigation", "t1117"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1117 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1117 Microsoft's Enhanced Mitigation Experience Toolkit (EMET) Attack Surface Reduction (ASR) feature can be used to block regsvr32.exe from being used to bypass whitelisting. (Citation: Secure Host Baseline EMET)"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1147", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1147 Hidden Users Mitigation", "tags": ["hidden users mitigation", "mitigation", "t1147"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1147 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1147 If the computer is domain joined, then group policy can help restrict the ability to create or hide users. Similarly, preventing the modification of the <code>/Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow</code> <code>Hide50"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1016", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1016 Vulnerability Scanning", "tags": ["m1016", "mitigation", "vulnerability scanning"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1016 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1016 Vulnerability scanning involves the automated or manual assessment of systems, applications, and networks to identify misconfigurations, unpatched software, or other security weaknesses. The process helps prioritize remed"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1009", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1009 Binary Padding Mitigation", "tags": ["binary padding mitigation", "mitigation", "t1009"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1009 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1009 Identify potentially malicious software that may be executed from a padded or otherwise obfuscated binary, and audit and/or block it by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLocker, (Citation: Windows"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1144", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1144 Gatekeeper Bypass Mitigation", "tags": ["gatekeeper bypass mitigation", "mitigation", "t1144"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1144 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1144 Other tools should be used to supplement Gatekeeper's functionality. Additionally, system settings can prevent applications from running that haven't been downloaded through the Apple Store which can help mitigate some of"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1013", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1013 Port Monitors Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "port monitors mitigation", "t1013"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1013 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1013 Identify and block potentially malicious software that may persist in this manner by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools capable of monitoring DLL loads by processes running under SYSTEM permissions."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1035", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1035 Limit Access to Resource Over Network", "tags": ["limit access to resource over network", "m1035", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1035 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1035 Restrict access to network resources, such as file shares, remote systems, and services, to only those users, accounts, or systems with a legitimate business requirement. This can include employing technologies like netwo"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1155", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1155 AppleScript Mitigation", "tags": ["applescript mitigation", "mitigation", "t1155"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1155 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1155 Require that all AppleScript be signed by a trusted developer ID before being executed - this will prevent random AppleScript code from executing (Citation: applescript signing). This subjects AppleScript code to the same"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1029", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1029 Remote Data Storage", "tags": ["m1029", "mitigation", "remote data storage"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1029 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1029 Remote Data Storage focuses on moving critical data, such as security logs and sensitive files, to secure, off-host locations to minimize unauthorized access, tampering, or destruction by adversaries. By leveraging remote"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1037", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1037 Filter Network Traffic", "tags": ["filter network traffic", "m1037", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1037 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1037 Employ network appliances and endpoint software to filter ingress, egress, and lateral network traffic. This includes protocol-based filtering, enforcing firewall rules, and blocking or restricting traffic based on predef"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1021", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1021 Restrict Web-Based Content", "tags": ["m1021", "mitigation", "restrict web-based content"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1021 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1021 Restricting web-based content involves enforcing policies and technologies that limit access to potentially malicious websites, unsafe downloads, and unauthorized browser behaviors. This can include URL filtering, downloa"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1130", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1130 Install Root Certificate Mitigation", "tags": ["install root certificate mitigation", "mitigation", "t1130"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1130 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1130 HTTP Public Key Pinning (HPKP) is one method to mitigate potential man-in-the-middle situations where and adversary uses a mis-issued or fraudulent certificate to intercept encrypted communications by enforcing use of an "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1033", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1033 Limit Software Installation", "tags": ["limit software installation", "m1033", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1033 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1033 Prevent users or groups from installing unauthorized or unapproved software to reduce the risk of introducing malicious or vulnerable applications. This can be achieved through allowlists, software restriction policies, e"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1169", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1169 Sudo Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "sudo mitigation", "t1169"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1169 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1169 The sudoers file should be strictly edited such that passwords are always required and that users can’t spawn risky processes as users with higher privilege. By requiring a password, even if an adversary can get terminal "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1079", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1079 Multilayer Encryption Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "multilayer encryption mitigation", "t1079"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1079 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1079 Network intrusion detection and prevention systems that use network signatures to identify traffic for specific adversary malware can be used to mitigate activity at the network level. Use of encryption protocols may make"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1493", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1493 Transmitted Data Manipulation Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1493", "transmitted data manipulation mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1493 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1493 Identify critical business and system processes that may be targeted by adversaries and work to secure communications related to those processes against tampering. Encrypt all important data flows to reduce the impact of "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1013", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1013 Application Developer Guidance", "tags": ["application developer guidance", "m1013", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1013 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1013 Application Developer Guidance focuses on providing developers with the knowledge, tools, and best practices needed to write secure code, reduce vulnerabilities, and implement secure design principles. By integrating secu"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1019", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1019 System Firmware Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "system firmware mitigation", "t1019"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1019 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1019 Prevent adversary access to privileged accounts or access necessary to perform this technique. Check the integrity of the existing BIOS or EFI to determine if it is vulnerable to modification. Patch the BIOS and EFI as ne"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1002", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1002 Data Compressed Mitigation", "tags": ["data compressed mitigation", "mitigation", "t1002"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1002 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1002 Identify unnecessary system utilities, third-party tools, or potentially malicious software that may be used to compress files, and audit and/or block them by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLoc"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1034", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1034 Limit Hardware Installation", "tags": ["limit hardware installation", "m1034", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1034 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1034 Prevent unauthorized users or groups from installing or using hardware, such as external drives, peripheral devices, or unapproved internal hardware components, by enforcing hardware usage policies and technical controls."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1017", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1017 User Training", "tags": ["m1017", "mitigation", "user training"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1017 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1017 User Training involves educating employees and contractors on recognizing, reporting, and preventing cyber threats that rely on human interaction, such as phishing, social engineering, and other manipulative techniques. C"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1022", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1022 Data Encrypted Mitigation", "tags": ["data encrypted mitigation", "mitigation", "t1022"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1022 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1022 Identify unnecessary system utilities, third-party tools, or potentially malicious software that may be used to encrypt files, and audit and/or block them by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools, like AppLock"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1052", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1052 User Account Control", "tags": ["m1052", "mitigation", "user account control"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1052 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1052 User Account Control (UAC) is a security feature in Microsoft Windows that prevents unauthorized changes to the operating system. UAC prompts users to confirm or provide administrator credentials when an action requires e"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1150", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1150 Plist Modification Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "plist modification mitigation", "t1150"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1150 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1150 Prevent plist files from being modified by users by making them read-only."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1028", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1028 Operating System Configuration", "tags": ["m1028", "mitigation", "operating system configuration"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1028 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1028 Operating System Configuration involves adjusting system settings and hardening the default configurations of an operating system (OS) to mitigate adversary exploitation and prevent abuse of system functionality. Proper O"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1077", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1077 Windows Admin Shares Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1077", "windows admin shares mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1077 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1077 Do not reuse local administrator account passwords across systems. Ensure password complexity and uniqueness such that the passwords cannot be cracked or guessed. Deny remote use of local admin credentials to log into sys"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1004", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1004 Winlogon Helper DLL Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1004", "winlogon helper dll mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1004 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1004 Limit the privileges of user accounts so that only authorized administrators can perform Winlogon helper changes. Identify and block potentially malicious software that may be executed through the Winlogon helper process "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1494", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1494 Runtime Data Manipulation Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "runtime data manipulation mitigation", "t1494"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1494 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1494 Identify critical business and system processes that may be targeted by adversaries and work to secure those systems against tampering. Prevent critical business and system processes from being replaced, overwritten, or r"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1183", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1183 Image File Execution Options Injection Mitigation", "tags": ["image file execution options injection mitigation", "mitigation", "t1183"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1183 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1183 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating all IFEO will likely have unintended side effec"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1186", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1186 Process Doppelgänging Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "process doppelgänging mitigation", "t1186"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1186 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1186 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls or patched since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating specific API calls will likely have "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1107", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1107 File Deletion Mitigation", "tags": ["file deletion mitigation", "mitigation", "t1107"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1107 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1107 Identify unnecessary system utilities, third-party tools, or potentially malicious software that may be used to delete files, and audit and/or block them by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools like AppLocker"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1089", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1089 Disabling Security Tools Mitigation", "tags": ["disabling security tools mitigation", "mitigation", "t1089"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1089 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1089 Ensure proper process, registry, and file permissions are in place to prevent adversaries from disabling or interfering with security services."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1196", "source": "