gs": ["mitigation", "t1028", "windows remote management mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1028 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1028 Disable the WinRM service. If the service is necessary, lock down critical enclaves with separate WinRM infrastructure, accounts, and permissions. Follow WinRM best practices on configuration of authentication methods and"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1053", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1053 Data Backup", "tags": ["data backup", "m1053", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1053 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1053 Data Backup involves taking and securely storing backups of data from end-user systems and critical servers. It ensures that data remains available in the event of system compromise, ransomware attacks, or other disruptio"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1160", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1160 Launch Daemon Mitigation", "tags": ["launch daemon mitigation", "mitigation", "t1160"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1160 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1160 Limit privileges of user accounts and remediate Privilege Escalation vectors so only authorized administrators can create new Launch Daemons."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1184", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1184 SSH Hijacking Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "ssh hijacking mitigation", "t1184"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1184 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1184 Ensure SSH key pairs have strong passwords and refrain from using key-store technologies such as ssh-agent unless they are properly protected. Ensure that all private keys are stored securely in locations where only the l"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1215", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1215 Kernel Modules and Extensions Mitigation", "tags": ["kernel modules and extensions mitigation", "mitigation", "t1215"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1215 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1215 Common tools for detecting Linux rootkits include: rkhunter (Citation: SourceForge rkhunter), chrootkit (Citation: Chkrootkit Main), although rootkits may be designed to evade certain detection tools. LKMs and Kernel exte"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1214", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1214 Credentials in Registry Mitigation", "tags": ["credentials in registry mitigation", "mitigation", "t1214"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1214 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1214 Do not store credentials within the Registry. Proactively search for credentials within Registry keys and attempt to remediate the risk. If necessary software must store credentials, then ensure those accounts have limite"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1038", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1038 Execution Prevention", "tags": ["execution prevention", "m1038", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1038 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1038 Prevent the execution of unauthorized or malicious code on systems by implementing application control, script blocking, and other execution prevention mechanisms. This ensures that only trusted and authorized code is exe"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1043", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1043 Credential Access Protection", "tags": ["credential access protection", "m1043", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1043 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1043 Credential Access Protection focuses on implementing measures to prevent adversaries from obtaining credentials, such as passwords, hashes, tokens, or keys, that could be used for unauthorized access. This involves restri"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1066", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1066 Indicator Removal from Tools Mitigation", "tags": ["indicator removal from tools mitigation", "mitigation", "t1066"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1066 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1066 Mitigation is difficult in instances like this because the adversary may have access to the system through another channel and can learn what techniques or tools are blocked by resident defenses. Exercising best practices"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1156", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1156 .bash_profile and .bashrc Mitigation", "tags": [".bash_profile and .bashrc mitigation", "mitigation", "t1156"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1156 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1156 Making these files immutable and only changeable by certain administrators will limit the ability for adversaries to easily create user level persistence."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1076", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1076 Remote Desktop Protocol Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "remote desktop protocol mitigation", "t1076"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1076 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1076 Disable the RDP service if it is unnecessary, remove unnecessary accounts and groups from Remote Desktop Users groups, and enable firewall rules to block RDP traffic between network security zones. Audit the Remote Deskto"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1171", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1171 LLMNR/NBT-NS Poisoning Mitigation", "tags": ["llmnr/nbt-ns poisoning mitigation", "mitigation", "t1171"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1171 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1171 Disable LLMNR and NetBIOS in local computer security settings or by group policy if they are not needed within an environment. (Citation: ADSecurity Windows Secure Baseline) Use host-based security software to block LLMNR"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1142", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1142 Keychain Mitigation", "tags": ["keychain mitigation", "mitigation", "t1142"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1142 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1142 The password for the user's login keychain can be changed from the user's login password. This increases the complexity for an adversary because they need to know an additional password."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1045", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1045 Code Signing", "tags": ["code signing", "m1045", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1045 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1045 Code Signing is a security process that ensures the authenticity and integrity of software by digitally signing executables, scripts, and other code artifacts. It prevents untrusted or malicious code from executing by ver"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1099", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1099 Timestomp Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1099", "timestomp mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1099 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1099 Mitigation of timestomping specifically is likely difficult. Efforts should be focused on preventing potentially malicious software from running. Identify and block potentially malicious software that may contain function"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1039", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1039 Environment Variable Permissions", "tags": ["environment variable permissions", "m1039", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1039 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1039 Restrict the modification of environment variables to authorized users and processes by enforcing strict permissions and policies. This ensures the integrity of environment variables, preventing adversaries from abusing o"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1164", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1164 Re-opened Applications Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "re-opened applications mitigation", "t1164"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1164 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1164 Holding the Shift key while logging in prevents apps from opening automatically (Citation: Re-Open windows on Mac). This feature can be disabled entirely with the following terminal command: <code>defaults write -g AppleP"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1128", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1128 Netsh Helper DLL Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "netsh helper dll mitigation", "t1128"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1128 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1128 Identify and block potentially malicious software that may persist in this manner by using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools capable of monitoring DLL loads by Windows utilities like AppLocker. (Citation: Window"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1172", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1172 Domain Fronting Mitigation", "tags": ["domain fronting mitigation", "mitigation", "t1172"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1172 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1172 If it is possible to inspect HTTPS traffic, the captures can be analyzed for connections that appear to be Domain Fronting. In order to use domain fronting, attackers will likely need to deploy additional tools to comprom"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1057", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1057 Data Loss Prevention", "tags": ["data loss prevention", "m1057", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1057 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1057 Data Loss Prevention (DLP) involves implementing strategies and technologies to identify, categorize, monitor, and control the movement of sensitive data within an organization. This includes protecting data formats indic"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1109", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1109 Component Firmware Mitigation", "tags": ["component firmware mitigation", "mitigation", "t1109"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1109 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1109 Prevent adversary access to privileged accounts or access necessary to perform this technique. Consider removing and replacing system components suspected of being compromised."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1025", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1025 Privileged Process Integrity", "tags": ["m1025", "mitigation", "privileged process integrity"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1025 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1025 Privileged Process Integrity focuses on defending highly privileged processes (e.g., system services, antivirus, or authentication processes) from tampering, injection, or compromise by adversaries. These processes often "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1188", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1188 Multi-hop Proxy Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "multi-hop proxy mitigation", "t1188"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1188 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1188 Traffic to known anonymity networks and C2 infrastructure can be blocked through the use of network black and white lists. It should be noted that this kind of blocking may be circumvented by other techniques like [Domain"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1161", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1161 LC_LOAD_DYLIB Addition Mitigation", "tags": ["lc_load_dylib addition mitigation", "mitigation", "t1161"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1161 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1161 Enforce that all binaries be signed by the correct Apple Developer IDs, and whitelist applications via known hashes. Binaries can also be baselined for what dynamic libraries they require, and if an app requires a new dyn"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1055", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1055 Do Not Mitigate", "tags": ["do not mitigate", "m1055", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1055 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1055 The Do Not Mitigate category highlights scenarios where attempting to mitigate a specific technique may inadvertently increase the organization's security risk or operational instability. This could happen due to the comp"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1056", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1056 Pre-compromise", "tags": ["m1056", "mitigation", "pre-compromise"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1056 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1056 Pre-compromise mitigations involve proactive measures and defenses implemented to prevent adversaries from successfully identifying and exploiting weaknesses during the Reconnaissance and Resource Development phases of an"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1073", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1073 DLL Side-Loading Mitigation", "tags": ["dll side-loading mitigation", "mitigation", "t1073"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1073 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1073 Update software regularly. Install software in write-protected locations. Use the program sxstrace.exe that is included with Windows along with manual inspection to check manifest files for side-loading vulnerabilities in"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1177", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1177 LSASS Driver Mitigation", "tags": ["lsass driver mitigation", "mitigation", "t1177"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1177 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1177 On Windows 8.1 and Server 2012 R2, enable LSA Protection by setting the Registry key <code>HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\RunAsPPL</code> to <code>dword:00000001</code>. (Citation: Microsoft LSA P"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1179", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1179 Hooking Mitigation", "tags": ["hooking mitigation", "mitigation", "t1179"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1179 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1179 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating all hooking will likely have unintended side ef"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1020", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1020 SSL/TLS Inspection", "tags": ["m1020", "mitigation", "ssl/tls inspection"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1020 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1020 SSL/TLS inspection involves decrypting encrypted network traffic to examine its content for signs of malicious activity. This capability is crucial for detecting threats that use encryption to evade detection, such as phi"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1093", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1093 Process Hollowing Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "process hollowing mitigation", "t1093"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1093 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1093 This type of attack technique cannot be easily mitigated with preventive controls since it is based on the abuse of operating system design features. For example, mitigating specific API calls will likely have unintended "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1046", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1046 Boot Integrity", "tags": ["boot integrity", "m1046", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1046 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1046 Boot Integrity ensures that a system starts securely by verifying the integrity of its boot process, operating system, and associated components. This mitigation focuses on leveraging secure boot mechanisms, hardware-root"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1154", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1154 Trap Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "t1154", "trap mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1154 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1154 Due to potential legitimate uses of trap commands, it's may be difficult to mitigate use of this technique."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1060", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1060 Out-of-Band Communications Channel", "tags": ["m1060", "mitigation", "out-of-band communications channel"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1060 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1060 Establish secure out-of-band communication channels to ensure the continuity of critical communications during security incidents, data integrity attacks, or in-network communication failures. Out-of-band communication re"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1173", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1173 Dynamic Data Exchange Mitigation", "tags": ["dynamic data exchange mitigation", "mitigation", "t1173"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1173 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1173 Registry keys specific to Microsoft Office feature control security can be set to disable automatic DDE/OLE execution. (Citation: Microsoft DDE Advisory Nov 2017) (Citation: BleepingComputer DDE Disabled in Word Dec 2017)"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1116", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1116 Code Signing Mitigation", "tags": ["code signing mitigation", "mitigation", "t1116"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1116 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1116 Process whitelisting and trusted publishers to verify authenticity of software can help prevent signed malicious or untrusted code from executing on a system. (Citation: NSA MS AppLocker) (Citation: TechNet Trusted Publis"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1501", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1501 Systemd Service Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "systemd service mitigation", "t1501"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1501 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1501 The creation and modification of systemd service unit files is generally reserved for administrators such as the Linux root user and other users with superuser privileges. Limit user access to system utilities such as sys"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1158", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1158 Hidden Files and Directories Mitigation", "tags": ["hidden files and directories mitigation", "mitigation", "t1158"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1158 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1158 Mitigation of this technique may be difficult and unadvised due to the the legitimate use of hidden files and directories."}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1030", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1030 Network Segmentation", "tags": ["m1030", "mitigation", "network segmentation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1030 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1030 Network segmentation involves dividing a network into smaller, isolated segments to control and limit the flow of traffic between devices, systems, and applications. By segmenting networks, organizations can reduce the at"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1019", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1019 Threat Intelligence Program", "tags": ["m1019", "mitigation", "threat intelligence program"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1019 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1019 A Threat Intelligence Program enables organizations to proactively identify, analyze, and act on cyber threats by leveraging internal and external data sources. The program supports decision-making processes, prioritizes "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1141", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1141 Input Prompt Mitigation", "tags": ["input prompt mitigation", "mitigation", "t1141"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1141 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1141 This technique exploits users' tendencies to always supply credentials when prompted, which makes it very difficult to mitigate. Use user training as a way to bring awareness and raise suspicion for potentially malicious "}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1060", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1060 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "registry run keys / startup folder mitigation", "t1060"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1060 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1060 Identify and block potentially malicious software that may be executed through run key or startup folder persistence using whitelisting (Citation: Beechey 2010) tools like AppLocker (Citation: Windows Commands JPCERT) (Ci"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1085", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1085 Rundll32 Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "rundll32 mitigation", "t1085"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1085 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1085 Microsoft's Enhanced Mitigation Experience Toolkit (EMET) Attack Surface Reduction (ASR) feature can be used to block methods of using rundll32.exe to bypass whitelisting. (Citation: Secure Host Baseline EMET)"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1193", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1193 Spearphishing Attachment Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "spearphishing attachment mitigation", "t1193"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1193 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1193 Network intrusion prevention systems and systems designed to scan and remove malicious email attachments can be used to block activity. Solutions can be signature and behavior based, but adversaries may construct attachme"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1027", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1027 Password Policies", "tags": ["m1027", "mitigation", "password policies"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1027 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1027 Set and enforce secure password policies for accounts to reduce the likelihood of unauthorized access. Strong password policies include enforcing password complexity, requiring regular password changes, and preventing pas"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-M1040", "source": "mitre", "title": "M1040 Behavior Prevention on Endpoint", "tags": ["behavior prevention on endpoint", "m1040", "mitigation"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** M1040 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/M1040 Behavior Prevention on Endpoint refers to the use of technologies and strategies to detect and block potentially malicious activities by analyzing the behavior of processes, files, API calls, and other endpoint events. Ra"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1191", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1191 CMSTP Mitigation", "tags": ["cmstp mitigation", "mitigation", "t1191"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1191 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1191 CMSTP.exe may not be necessary within a given environment (unless using it for VPN connection installation). Consider using application whitelisting configured to block execution of CMSTP.exe if it is not required for a g"}, {"id": "mitre-attack-T1058", "source": "mitre", "title": "T1058 Service Registry Permissions Weakness Mitigation", "tags": ["mitigation", "service registry permissions weakness mitigation", "t1058"], "summary": "**ATT&CK ID:** T1058 **Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/mitigations/T1058 Ensure proper permissions are set for Registry hives to prevent users from m