--- Binary analysis frameworks give you powerful ways to automate tasks that would be almost impossible to do manually. Binary analysis frameworks typicall"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0051", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0051", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0051", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Tampering and Runtime Instrumentation platform: generic --- First, we'll look at some simple ways to modify and instrument mobile apps. _Tampering_ means making patches or runtime changes t"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0071", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0071", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0071", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Retrieving Strings platform: generic --- Strings are always a good starting point while analyzing a binary, as they provide context to the associated code. For instance, an error log string"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0119", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0119", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0119", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Intercepting HTTP Traffic by Hooking Network APIs at the Application Layer platform: generic --- Depending on your goal while testing the app, sometimes it is enough to monitor the traffic "}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0120", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0120", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0120", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Intercepting HTTP Traffic Using an Interception Proxy platform: generic --- Interception proxies are the most common method for intercepting mobile app traffic. They work by setting up a pr"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0121", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0121", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0121", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Intercepting Non-HTTP Traffic Using an Interception Proxy platform: generic --- Interception proxies such as @MASTG-TOOL-0077 and @MASTG-TOOL-0079 won't show non-HTTP traffic, because they "}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0122", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0122", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0122", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Passive Eavesdropping platform: generic --- This method involves passively capturing network traffic using tools such as @MASTG-TOOL-0081, @MASTG-TOOL-0080, or @MASTG-TOOL-0075. It is usefu"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0123", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0123", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0123", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Achieving a MITM Position via ARP Spoofing platform: generic --- When proxy-based interception fails due to non-HTTP protocols or non-proxy-aware apps, **ARP Spoofing** can be used to redir"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0124", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0124", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0124", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Achieving a MITM Position Using a Rogue Access Point platform: generic --- To achieve a [Machine-in-the-Middle (MITM)](../../Document/0x04f-Testing-Network-Communication.md#intercepting-net"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0125", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0125", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0125", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Intercepting Xamarin Traffic platform: generic status: deprecated --- !!! note Xamarin has been [End of Support (EoS)](https://dotnet.microsoft.com/en-us/apps/xamarin) since May 1, 2024 and"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0052", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0052", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0052", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Accessing the Device Shell platform: ios --- One of the most common tasks in app testing is accessing the device shell. In this section, we'll see how to access the iOS shell both remotely "}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0053", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0053", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0053", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Host-Device Data Transfer platform: ios --- There are various scenarios where you may need to transfer data between the iOS device or app sandbox and your host computer. The following secti"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0054", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0054", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0054", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Obtaining and Extracting Apps platform: ios --- ## Getting the IPA File from an OTA Distribution Link During development, apps are sometimes provided to testers via over-the-air (OTA) distr"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0055", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0055", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0055", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Launching a Repackaged App in Debug Mode platform: ios --- If you've repackaged an application with a Frida Gadget, or if you want to attach @MASTG-TOOL-0057 to the application, you have to"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0056", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0056", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0056", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Installing Apps platform: ios --- Installing an application outside the Apple App Store is called sideloading. There are several ways of sideloading, as described below. On iOS, the install"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0057", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0057", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0057", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Listing Installed Apps platform: ios --- When targeting apps installed on the device, you'll first need to determine the correct bundle identifier for the application you want to analyze. Y"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0058", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0058", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0058", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Exploring the App Package platform: ios --- Once you have the application package name you want to target, you may want to start gathering information about it. First, retrieve the @MASTG-A"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0059", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0059", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0059", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Accessing App Data Directories platform: ios --- ## Using @MASTG-TOOL-0138 (Jailbroken Devices Only) Before you can access the app directories, you need to know where they are located in th"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0060", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0060", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0060", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Monitoring System Logs platform: ios --- Many apps emit runtime messages through more than one output path. On iOS, it is important to distinguish between: - **System logs / Unified Logging"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0061", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0061", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0061", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Dumping KeyChain Data platform: ios --- Dumping Keychain data can be done with multiple tools, but not all of them work on all iOS versions. As is often the case, try different tools or con"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0062", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0062", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0062", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Basic Network Monitoring/Sniffing platform: ios --- You can remotely sniff all traffic in real-time on iOS by [creating a Remote Virtual Interface](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/95554"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0063", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0063", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0063", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Setting up an Interception Proxy platform: ios --- @MASTG-TOOL-0077 is an integrated platform for security testing mobile and web applications. Its tools work together seamlessly to support"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0064", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0064", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0064", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Bypassing Certificate Pinning platform: ios --- Some applications will implement SSL Pinning, which prevents the application from accepting an intercepted certificate as valid. This means t"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0065", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0065", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0065", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Reverse Engineering iOS Apps platform: ios --- iOS reverse engineering is a mixed bag. On the one hand, apps programmed in Objective-C and Swift can be disassembled nicely. In Objective-C, "}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0066", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0066", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0066", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Static Analysis on iOS platform: ios --- The preferred method for statically analyzing iOS apps is to use the original Xcode project files. Ideally, you can compile and debug the app to qui"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0067", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0067", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0067", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Dynamic Analysis on iOS platform: ios --- Jailbreaking a device simplifies many aspects of dynamic analysis. It provides privileged access and removes code signing restrictions, enabling th"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0068", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0068", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0068", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Disassembling Native Code platform: ios --- Because Objective-C and Swift are fundamentally different, the programming language used to write the app affects the feasibility of reverse engi"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0070", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0070", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0070", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Extracting Information from the Application Binary platform: ios --- You can use @MASTG-TOOL-0073 to retrieve information about the binary, including the architecture, shared libraries, cla"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0073", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0073", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0073", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Information Gathering - API Usage platform: ios --- The iOS platform provides many built-in libraries for common application functionality, including cryptography, Bluetooth, NFC, networkin"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0074", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0074", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0074", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Information Gathering - Network Communication platform: ios --- Most of the apps you might encounter connect to remote endpoints. Even before you perform any dynamic analysis (e.g., traffic"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0076", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0076", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0076", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Reviewing Disassembled Objective-C and Swift Code platform: ios --- In this section, we will manually examine an iOS application's binary code and perform static analysis. Manual analysis c"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0077", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0077", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0077", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Reviewing Disassembled Native Code platform: ios --- Analyzing disassembled native code requires a good understanding of the calling conventions and instructions used by the underlying plat"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0078", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0078", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0078", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Automated Static Analysis platform: ios --- Several automated tools for analyzing iOS apps are available; most are commercial. The free and open-source tools @MASTG-TOOL-0035 and @MASTG-TOO"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0079", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0079", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0079", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Obtaining a Developer Provisioning Profile platform: ios --- The _provisioning profile_ is a plist file signed by Apple that adds your code-signing certificate to the list of accepted certi"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0080", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0080", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0080", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Get Open Files platform: ios --- `lsof` is a powerful command that provides a wealth of information about running processes. It can provide a list of all open files, including streams, netw"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0081", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0081", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0081", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Get Open Connections platform: ios --- The `lsof` command, when invoked with option `-i`, gives the list of open network ports for all active processes on the device. To get a list of open "}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0082", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0082", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0082", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Extracting Bundled Libraries platform: ios --- This technique describes how to identify dynamic libraries, [framework](https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/technotes/tn2435/_index.ht"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0084", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0084", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0084", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Debugging platform: ios --- Coming from a Linux background, you'd expect the `ptrace` system call to be as powerful as you're used to, but, for some reason, Apple decided to leave it incomp"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0085", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0085", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0085", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Execution Tracing platform: ios --- Tracing involves recording the information about a program's execution. In contrast to Android, iOS offers limited options for tracing various aspects of"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0086", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0086", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0086", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Method Tracing platform: ios --- Intercepting Objective-C methods is a useful iOS security testing technique. For example, you may be interested in data storage operations or network reques"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0087", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0087", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0087", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Native Code Tracing platform: ios --- As discussed earlier in this chapter, iOS applications can also contain native code (C/C++) that can be traced with the `frida-trace` CLI. For example,"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0088", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0088", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0088", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Emulation-based Analysis platform: ios --- ## iOS Simulator Apple provides a simulator app within Xcode that provides an _iOS device-looking_ user interface for iPhone, iPad, or Apple Watch"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0089", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0089", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0089", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Symbolic Execution platform: ios --- TODO: Currently don't have this, but we do have this section below, which does talk about Angr and a small intro referring to Android You can find an in"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0090", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0090", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0090", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Injecting Frida Gadget into an IPA Automatically platform: ios --- If you want to enable dynamic testing with Frida but don't have access to a jailbroken device, you can patch and repackage"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0091", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0091", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0091", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Injecting Libraries into an IPA Manually platform: ios --- This technique allows you to inject arbitrary libraries into an IPA file. After injecting the library, you'll have to install the "}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0092", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0092", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0092", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Signing IPA files platform: ios --- To install an IPA file on a non-jailbroken device, the IPA must have a valid signature. On a jailbroken device, this is not required after installing @MA"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0094", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0094", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0094", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Getting Loaded Classes and Methods dynamically platform: ios --- In the Frida REPL Objective-C runtime, the `ObjC` command can be used to access information within the running app. Within t"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0095", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0095", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0095", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Method Hooking platform: ios --- ## Frida In @MASTG-TECH-0085, we used frida-trace when navigating to a website in Safari and found that the `initWithURL:` method is called to initialize a "}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0096", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0096", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0096", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Process Exploration platform: ios --- When testing an app, process exploration can provide the tester with deep insights into the app's process memory. It can be achieved via runtime instru"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0097", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0097", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0097", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Runtime Reverse Engineering platform: ios --- Runtime reverse engineering is the on-the-fly version of reverse engineering, where you don't have the binary on your host computer. Instead, y"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0098", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0098", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0098", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Patching React Native Apps platform: ios --- If the [React Native](https://reactnative.dev/ \"React Native\") framework has been used for development, the main application code is in the file"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0110", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0110", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0110", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Intercepting Flutter HTTPS Traffic platform: ios --- Flutter is an open-source UI software development kit (SDK) created by Google. It is used for building natively compiled applications fo"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0111", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0111", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0111", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Extracting Entitlements from MachO Binaries platform: ios --- Several tools can be used to extract entitlements from Mach-O binaries on iOS. This is useful for security assessments, as enti"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0112", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0112", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0112", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Obtaining the Code Signature Format Version platform: ios --- To extract the code signature format version from a signed binary, you can use @MASTG-TOOL-0114. The version is obtained by cal"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0113", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0113", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0113", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Obtaining Debugging Symbols platform: ios --- To retrieve debug symbols from iOS binaries, you can use @MASTG-TOOL-0073, @MASTG-TOOL-0121, or @MASTG-TOOL-0041 to inspect all the app binarie"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0114", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0114", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0114", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Demangling Symbols platform: ios --- To make some identifiers unique within the program, the compiler processes their symbol names. This process is called \"name mangling\" or simply \"manglin"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0118", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0118", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0118", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Obtaining Compiler-Provided Security Features platform: ios --- The iOS compiler provides several security features that can be enabled during compilation (see @MASTG-KNOW-0061). These feat"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0132", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0132", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0132", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Software Composition Analysis (SCA) of iOS Dependencies by Creating a SBOM platform: ios --- You can use @MASTG-TOOL-0134 to generate a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) in the CycloneDX fo"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0133", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0133", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0133", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Software Composition Analysis (SCA) of iOS Dependencies by Scanning Package Manager Artifacts platform: ios --- iOS has several dependency managers, where the most popular are: - [Carthage]"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0134", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0134", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0134", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Monitoring the Pasteboard platform: ios --- You can monitor the pasteboard to check for sensitive data being copied to it. This is useful for detecting potential data leaks or misuse of the"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0135", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0135", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0135", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Bypassing Biometric Authentication platform: ios --- This technique can typically be used to bypass biometric authentication in apps that perform it by only returning a _success_ or _failur"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0136", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0136", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0136", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Retrieving PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy Files platform: ios --- iOS apps can include [privacy manifest files](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/bundleresources/privacy-manifest-files) that"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0137", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0137", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0137", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Analyzing PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy Files platform: ios --- Once you've got a privacy manifest as indicated in @MASTG-TECH-0136, you can proceed to analyze it. Let's use the `SocialApp.app/Priv"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0138", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0138", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0138", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Convert Plist Files to JSON platform: ios --- You can convert Plist files, such as `Info.plist` or `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` (which is a Plist file despite the '.xcprivacy' extension), to JSO"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0139", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0139", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0139", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Attach to WKWebView platform: ios --- ## Safari Web Inspector Enabling the [Safari Web Inspector](https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/AppleApplications/Conceptual/Safa"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0146", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0146", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0146", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Dynamic Analysis on Non-Jailbroken Devices platform: ios --- If you do not have a jailbroken test device, you can still perform dynamic analysis on a non-jailbroken iOS device by patching a"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0147", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0147", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0147", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Patching platform: ios --- Making small changes to an iOS app can help overcome common obstacles during security testing and reverse engineering. On iOS, two issues in particular happen reg"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0149", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0149", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0149", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Validating ATS TLS Settings at Runtime Using nscurl platform: ios --- [`nscurl`](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/identifying-the-source-of-blocked-connections) is a macOS"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0152", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0152", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0152", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Bypassing Jailbreak Detection platform: ios --- Bypassing jailbreak detection involves identifying the specific checks an app performs at runtime and then nullifying them, either through au"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0153", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0153", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0153", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Retrieving Info.plist Files platform: ios --- The `Info.plist` file is the primary property list configuration file included in every iOS app bundle. It contains key-value pairs that descri"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0154", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0154", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0154", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Analyzing Info.plist Files platform: ios --- Once you have the `Info.plist` file as described in @MASTG-TECH-0153, you can analyze its contents to inspect security-relevant settings such as"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0155", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0155", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0155", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Analyzing the ATS Configuration platform: ios --- App Transport Security (ATS) settings are declared under the `NSAppTransportSecurity` key in the `Info.plist` file. ATS controls the securi"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0158", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0158", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0158", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Extracting Loaded Libraries platform: ios --- This technique describes how to enumerate the dynamic libraries loaded into memory by a running iOS app. Unlike @MASTG-TECH-0082, which identif"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0166", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0166", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0166", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Identifying Custom URL Scheme Registrations in iOS Apps platform: ios --- iOS apps declare custom URL schemes in the `Info.plist` file and handle incoming URLs through delegate methods. Thi"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0167", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0167", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0167", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Monitoring UIActivity Data Sharing platform: ios --- When an app shares data through the system Share Sheet (see @MASTG-KNOW-0081), you can inspect the shared items, custom activities, and "}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0168", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0168", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0168", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Monitoring UIActivity Data Receiving platform: ios --- Use this technique to observe how an app handles files handed to it via the system Share Sheet, AirDrop, or Mail (see @MASTG-KNOW-0081"}, {"id": "owasp-mastg-mastg-tech-0169", "source": "owasp", "title": "OWASP MASTG Technique: MASTG TECH 0169", "tags": ["mastg", "mastg tech 0169", "mobile", "owasp", "technique"], "summary": "--- title: Opening Deep Links platform: ios --- To test how an iOS app handles deep links, you c