 \nOSSTMM \n4\n\n \nThe most significant revision in the methodology's 26-year history. OSSTMM 4 is restructured as a series of Facets: one Foundation volume carrying the theory, measurement model, and workflow, plus channel-specific testing volumes.\n\n \nOSSTMM 4 is formally derived from quantum information theory and thermodynamics. The companion paper \nSecurity as a Natural Law\n (Herzog, 2026) provides the physics. The Facets series provides the operational translation. Security is no longer treated as a best-practice checklist but as a measurable physical property of systems.\n\n \nGold members get early access to Facets as they are released.\n\n \n\n \nBecome a Gold Member\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nPhysics-Based Foundation.\n Security is formally derived from quantum information theory and thermodynamics. Two fundamental forces drive every security outcome: entropy (disorder) and latency (structural friction imposed by the environment).\n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nFive Attributes (5PP).\n Characteristics, Context, Force, Resources, and Narrative replace the previous security model with properties derived from quantum information theory.\n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nControl Lattice (5&times;3 = 15 Controls).\n Expanded from V3. Each control has a precise, measurable definition mapped to a specific attribute and temporal phase.\n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nThree Temporal Phases.\n Intent (before), React (during), Resolve (after). The energy cost of each phase differs: Intent is cheapest, React is most expensive, Resolve acts backward in time.\n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nTrust Controls.\n 15 Trust Controls manage the assumptions systems operate on when security controls are reduced. AI agents are the headline use case.\n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nAttack Surface Mapping.\n The Inverse MITRE concept: maps control gaps to exploitable paths instead of cataloging attacker techniques.\n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nRAV Reformulated.\n New formula using log base-10 normalization. Per-dimension scoring. JSON/spreadsheet companion for tool integration.\n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nNew Channels.\n Web, Cloud, Mobile, AI, Source Code, OT/ICS, IoT, AI/Agents, PQE, and more join the original five.\n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nCompliance Mapping.\n NIS2, DORA, EU AI Act, GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS, ISO 27001, NIST CSF, and other frameworks organized by industry.\n\n \n\n&#9670;\n\nWritten for AI.\n Methodology sections are structured for AI parsing as primary consumer. Definitions are machine-parseable. Test modules are discrete, executable instruction sets.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \nSecurity as a Natural Law\n\n \nThe theoretical foundation behind OSSTMM 4.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \nO4's theoretical foundation comes from the peer-reviewed physics paper \nSecurity as a Natural Law\n (Herzog, 2026), which presents the hypothesis that security properties derive from quantum information theory and thermodynamics. Rather than treating security as a human construct of policies and best practices, the paper demonstrates that security behaviors emerge from the same physical laws that govern waves and information at the quantum level.\n\n \nThe five attributes that underpin the entire OSSTMM 4 framework (Characteristics, Context, Force, Resources, Narrative) are derived from five quantum information properties. The two fundamental forces that determine every security outcome (entropy and latency) are thermodynamic realities, not management abstractions. This means OSSTMM test results measure actual physical properties of a system's security posture, not compliance with arbitrary checklists.\n\n \nThe practical consequence: security testing becomes reproducible, comparable, and falsifiable across any channel, any organization, any regulatory framework. A RAV score computed in one engagement means the same thing in every other engagement because it measures the same underlying physics.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \nThe Facets\n\n \nOSSTMM 4 is published as one Foundation volume which carries the theory, measurement, and workflow and additional channel-specific Facets that carry the testing methodology. Each Facet stands alone as a testable specification.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \nFoundation\n", "isecom-osstmm-1": "# ISECOM OSSTMM: Facet 1: Foundation\n\nFacet 1: Foundation\n\n \nThe master volume. Carries the load-bearing theory, the Attack Surface Mapping and Rules of Engagement, the RAV measurement model, the STAR reporting format, compliance mapping, and the four-phase workflow that every channel-specific Facet references. This is where OSSTMM starts.\n\n \n25 chapters + 6 appendices &middot; 102 pages &middot; Includes RAV calculation companion and compliance mapping to NIS2, DORA, EU AI Act, GDPR, HIPAA, PCI DSS, ISO 27001, NIST CSF\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \nOriginal Channels\n", "isecom-osstmm-11": "# ISECOM OSSTMM: Facet 10\n\nFacet 10\n\n \nAI Security Testing\n\n \nThe Model, Agent, and Retrieval-Augmented-System Channel\n\n \nAI-system engagements: model prompt/context surfaces, training-data provenance, retrieval augmentation, agent action envelopes, and the new attack primitives (jailbreak, prompt injection, tool-use exploitation) specific to generative and agentic AI. 17 test modules including hallucination propagation, memory manipulation detection, and model replication testing.\n", "isecom-osstmm-14": "# ISECOM OSSTMM: Facet 13\n\nFacet 13\n\n \nIoT Security Testing\n\n \nThe Constrained-Device and Embedded-System Channel\n\n \nConstrained devices and embedded systems: firmware, update mechanisms, sensor integrity, and the security of devices too small to run traditional defenses.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \nGet Gold Early Access as Facets Are Released\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \nTools and reporting\n\n \nEverything you need to run an OSSTMM-compliant security test.\n\n \n\n \n\n \n\n \nRAV Calculator\n\n \nReformulated for V4. Log base-10 normalization, per-dimension scoring, JSON/spreadsheet companion for tool integration.