te access and execution on target web servers.(Citation: ANSSI Sandworm January 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1222.002 Linux and Mac Permissions\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n- T1213.006 Databases\n- T1110.001 Password Guessing\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1084": "# S1084 QUIETEXIT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1084\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1084\n\n[QUIETEXIT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1084) is a novel backdoor, based on the open-source Dropbear SSH client-server software, that has been used by [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) since at least 2021. [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) has deployed [QUIETEXIT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1084) on opaque network appliances that typically don't support antivirus or endpoint detection and response tools within a victim environment.(Citation: Mandiant APT29 Eye Spy Email Nov 22)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1090.002 External Proxy\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1071 Application Layer Protocol\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0205": "# S0205 Naid\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0205\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0205\n\n[Naid](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0205) is a trojan used by [Elderwood](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0066) to open a backdoor on compromised hosts. (Citation: Symantec Elderwood Sept 2012) (Citation: Symantec Naid June 2012)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1133": "# S1133 Apostle\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1133\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1133\n\n[Apostle](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1133) is malware that has functioned as both a wiper and, in more recent versions, as ransomware.  [Apostle](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1133) is written in .NET and shares various programming and functional overlaps with [IPsec Helper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1132).(Citation: SentinelOne Agrius 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1561.001 Disk Content Wipe\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0180": "# S0180 Volgmer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0180\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0180\n\n[Volgmer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0180) is a backdoor Trojan designed to provide covert access to a compromised system. It has been used since at least 2013 to target the government, financial, automotive, and media industries. Its primary delivery mechanism is suspected to be spearphishing. (Citation: US-CERT Volgmer Nov 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1027.011 Fileless Storage\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1027.