ed by multiple Chinese APT groups.(Citation: Novetta-Axiom)(Citation: ThreatConnect Anthem) Both Windows and Linux variants have been observed.(Citation: Fidelis Turbo)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1218.010 Regsvr32\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1571 Non-Standard Port\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1012 Query Registry\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1180": "# S1180 BlackByte Ransomware\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1180\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1180\n\n[BlackByte Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1180) is uniquely associated with [BlackByte](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1043) operations. [BlackByte Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1180) used a common key for infections, allowing for the creation of a universal decryptor.(Citation: Trustwave BlackByte 2021)(Citation: FBI BlackByte 2022) [BlackByte Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1180) was replaced in [BlackByte](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1043) operations by [BlackByte 2.0 Ransomware](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1181) by 2023.(Citation: Microsoft BlackByte 2023)(Citation: Cisco BlackByte 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1689 Downgrade Attack\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1222.001 Windows Permissions\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1614.001 System Language Discovery\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9028": "# S9028 PHPsert\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9028\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9028\n\n[PHPsert](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9028) is a webshell used to execute PHP code that has been in use since at least 2023 against targets in Japan, Singapore, Peru, Taiwan, Iran, Republic of Korea, and the Philippines. [PHPsert](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9028) is not typically deployed as a standalone but integrated into web content such as text editors and content management systems.(Citation: sentinelone operationDigitalEye Dec 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0627": "# S0627 SodaMaster\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0627\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0627\n\n[SodaMaster](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0627) is a fileless malware used by [menuPass](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0045) to download and execute payloads since at least 2020.(Citation: Securelist APT10 March 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1012 Query Registry\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0009": "# S0009 Hikit\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0009\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0009\n\n[Hikit](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0009) is malware that has been used by [Axiom](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0001) for late-stage persistence and exfiltration after the initial compromise.(Citation: Novetta-Axiom)(Citation: FireEye Hikit Rootkit)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1566 Phishing\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1553.006 Code Signing Policy Modification\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1553.004 Install Root Certificate\n- T1014 Rootkit\n- T1090.001 Internal Proxy\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1183": "# S1183 StrelaStealer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1183\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1183\n\n[StrelaStealer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1183) is an information stealer malware variant first identified in November 2022 and active through late 2024. [StrelaStealer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1183) focuses on the automated identification, collection, and exfiltration of email credentials from email clients such as Outlook and Thunderbird.(Citation: DCSO StrelaStealer 2022)(Citation: PaloAlto StrelaStealer 2024)(Citation: Fortgale StrelaStealer 2023)(Citation: IBM StrelaStealer 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1001 Data Obfuscation\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1027.016 Junk Code Insertion\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1622 Debugger Evasion\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1036.003 Rename Legitimate Utilities\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1497 Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1027.015 Compression\n-