://attack.mitre.org/software/S0241) has been used in attacks targeting financial institutions in Poland, Mexico, Uruguay, the United Kingdom, and Chile. It was also seen used against organizations related to telecommunications, management consulting, information technology, insurance, aviation, and education. [RATANKBA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0241) has a graphical user interface to allow the attacker to issue jobs to perform on the infected machines. (Citation: Lazarus RATANKBA) (Citation: RATANKBA)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1042": "# S1042 SUGARDUMP\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1042\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1042\n\n[SUGARDUMP](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1042) is a proprietary browser credential harvesting tool that was used by UNC3890 during the [C0010](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0010) campaign. The first known [SUGARDUMP](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1042) version was used since at least early 2021, a second SMTP C2 version was used from late 2021-early 2022, and a third HTTP C2 variant was used since at least April 2022.(Citation: Mandiant UNC3890 Aug 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1217 Browser Information Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1071.003 Mail Protocols\n- T1560.003 Archive via Custom Method\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1207": "# S1207 XLoader\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1207\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1207\n\n[XLoader](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1207) is an infostealer malware in use since at least 2016. Previously known and sometimes still referred to as Formbook, [XLoader](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1207) is a Malware as a Service (MaaS) known for stealing data from web browsers, email clients and File Transfer Protocol (FTP) applications.(Citation: Zscaler XLoader 2025)(Citation: ANY.RUN XLoader 2023)(Citation: CheckPoint XLoader 2022)(Citation: Acronis XLoader 2021)(Citation: Google XLoader 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1555 Credentials from Password Stores\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1539 Steal Web Session Cookie\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1059.010 AutoHotKey & AutoIT\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1203 Exploitation for Client Execution\n- T1497 Virtualization/Sandbox Evasion\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1185 Browser Session Hijacking\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1622 Debugger Evasion\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1055.004 Asynchronous Procedure Call\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1583.001 Domains\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0157": "# S0157 SOUNDBITE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0157\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/softw