.mitre.org/groups/G0016)'s SolarWinds cyber operation but determined to be unrelated. Subsequent analysis suggests [SUPERNOVA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0578) may have been used by the China-based threat group SPIRAL.(Citation: Guidepoint SUPERNOVA Dec 2020)(Citation: Unit42 SUPERNOVA Dec 2020)(Citation: SolarWinds Advisory Dec 2020)(Citation: CISA Supernova Jan 2021)(Citation: Microsoft Analyzing Solorigate Dec 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1203 Exploitation for Client Execution\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0016": "# S0016 P2P ZeuS\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0016\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0016\n\n[P2P ZeuS](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0016) is a closed-source fork of the leaked version of the ZeuS botnet. It presents improvements over the leaked version, including a peer-to-peer architecture. (Citation: Dell P2P ZeuS)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1001.001 Junk Data\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0437": "# S0437 Kivars\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0437\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0437\n\n[Kivars](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0437) is a modular remote access tool (RAT), derived from the Bifrost RAT, that was used by [BlackTech](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0098) in a 2010 campaign.(Citation: TrendMicro BlackTech June 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1564.003 Hidden Window\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1021 Remote Services\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0693": "# S0693 CaddyWiper\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0693\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0693\n\n[CaddyWiper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0693) is a destructive data wiper that has been used in attacks against organizations in Ukraine since at least March 2022.(Citation: ESET CaddyWiper March 2022)(Citation: Cisco CaddyWiper March 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1222.001 Windows Permissions\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1561.002 Disk Structure Wipe\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0687": "# S0687 Cyclops Blink\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0687\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0687\n\n[Cyclops Blink](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0687) is a modular malware that has been used in widespread campaigns by [Sandworm Team](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0034) since at least 2019 to target Small/Home Office (SOHO) network devices, including WatchGuard and Asus. [Cyclops Blink](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0687) is assessed to be a replacement for [VPNFilter](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1010), a similar platform targeting network devices.(Citation: NCSC Cyclops Blink February 2022)(Citation: NCSC CISA Cyclops Blink Advisory February 2022)(Citation: Trend Micro Cyclops Blink March 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1542.002 Component Firmware\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1132.002 Non-Standard Encoding\n- T1090.003 Multi-hop Proxy\n- T1559 Inter-Process Communication\n- T1686.002 Network Device Firewall\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1037.004 RC Scripts\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1572 Protocol Tunneling\n- T1571 Non-Standard Port\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0012": "# S0012 PoisonIvy\n\n**ATT&CK ID:*