//attack.mitre.org/software/S0154) loaders used by [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016) since at least 2021.(Citation: MSTIC Nobelium Toolset May 2021)(Citation: SentinelOne NobleBaron June 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1036 Masquerading\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0336": "# S0336 NanoCore\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0336\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0336\n\n[NanoCore](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0336) is a modular remote access tool developed in .NET that can be used to spy on victims and steal information. It has been used by threat actors since 2013.(Citation: DigiTrust NanoCore Jan 2017)(Citation: Cofense NanoCore Mar 2018)(Citation: PaloAlto NanoCore Feb 2016)(Citation: Unit 42 Gorgon Group Aug 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1686 Disable or Modify System Firewall\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0467": "# S0467 TajMahal\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0467\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0467\n\n[TajMahal](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0467) is a multifunctional spying framework that has been in use since at least 2014. [TajMahal](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0467) is comprised of two separate packages, named Tokyo and Yokohama, and can deploy up to 80 plugins.(Citation: Kaspersky TajMahal April 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1560.002 Archive via Library\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1020 Automated Exfiltration\n- T1129 Shared Modules\n- T1539 Steal Web Session Cookie\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1025 Data from Removable Media\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0435": "# S0435 PLEAD\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0435\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0435\n\n[PLEAD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0435) is a remote access tool (RAT) and downloader used by [BlackTech](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0098) in targeted attacks in East Asia including Taiwan, Japan, and Hong Kong.(Citation: TrendMicro BlackTech June 2017)(Citation: JPCert PLEAD Downloader June 2018) [PLEAD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0435) has also been referred to as [TSCookie](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0436), though more recent reporting indicates likely separation between the two. [PLEAD](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0435) was observed in use as early as March 2017.(Citation: JPCert TSCookie March 2018)(Citation: JPCert PLEAD Downloader June 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1555 Credentials from Password Stores\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1001.001 Junk Data\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1204.001 Malicious Link\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1010 Application Window Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1148": "# S1148 Raccoon Stealer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1148\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1148\n\n[Raccoon Stealer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1148) is an information stealer malware family active since at least 2019 as a malware-as-a-service offering sold in underground forums. [Raccoon Stealer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1148) has experienced two periods of activity across two variants, from 2019 to March 2022, then resurfacing in a revised version in June 2022.(Citation: S2W Racoon 2022)(Citation: Sekoia Raccoon1 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1020 Automated Exfiltration\n- T1213 Data from Information Repositories\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1027.007 Dynamic API Resolution\n- T1195 Supply Chain Compromise\n- T1614 System Location Discovery\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1539 Steal Web Session Cookie\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1132": "# S1132 IPsec Helper\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1132\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1132\n\n[IPsec Helper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1132) is a post-exploitation remote access tool linked to [Agrius](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1030) operations. This malware shares significant programming and functional overlaps with [Apostle](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1133) ransomware, also linked to [Agrius](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1030). [IPsec Helper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1132) provides basic remote access tool functionality such as uploading files from victim systems, running commands, and deploying additional payloads.(Citation: SentinelOne Agrius 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1070 Indicator Removal\n- T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1070.009 Clear Persistence\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0187": "# S0187 Daserf\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0187\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0187\n\n[Daserf](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0187) is a backdoor that has been used to spy on and steal from Japanese, South Korean, Russian, Singaporean, and Chinese victims. Researchers have identified versions written in both Visual C and Delphi. (Citation: Trend Micro Daserf Nov 2017) (Citation: Secureworks BRONZE BUTLER Oct 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1001.002 Steganography\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1003.001 LSASS Memory\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1027.005 Indicator Removal from Tools\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0597": "# S0597 GoldFinder\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0597\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0597\n\n[GoldFinder](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0597) is a custom HTTP tracer tool written in Go that logs the route a packet takes between a compromised network and a C2 server. It can be used to inform  threat actors of potential points of discovery or logging of their actions, including C2 related to other malware. [GoldFinder](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0597) was discovered in early 2021 during an investigation into the [SolarWinds Compromise](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0024) by [APT29](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0016).(Citation: MSTIC NOBELIUM Mar 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1016.001 Internet Connection Discovery\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0335": "# S0335 Carbon\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0335\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0335\n\n[Carbon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0335) is a sophisticated, second-stage backdoor and framework that can be used to steal sensitive information from victims. [Carbon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0335) has been selectively used by [Turla](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0010) to target government and foreign affairs-related organizations in Central Asia.(Citation: ESET Carbon Mar 2017)(Citation: Securelist Turla Oct 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1102 Web Service\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1069 Permission Groups Discovery\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Unencrypted Non-C2 Protocol\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9012": "# S9012 TRAILBLAZE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9012\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9012\n\n[TRAILBLAZE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9012) is an in-memory dropper used to deploy the passive backdoor [BRUSHFIRE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9011). First reported in March 2025, TRAILBLAZE has been observed in operations attributed to People's Republic of China (PRC) state-sponsored affiliated actors, including UNC5221 and SYLVANITE. (Citation: Dragos SYLVANITE MuddyWater Electrum March 2026)(Citation: Google UNC5221 Ivanti April 2025)(Citation: Picus Security UNC5221 Ivanti May 2025)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1106 Native API\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0397": "# S0397 LoJax\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0397\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0397\n\n[LoJax](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0397) is a UEFI rootkit used by [APT28](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0007) to persist remote access software on targeted systems.(Citation: ESET LoJax Sept 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1564.004 NTFS File Attributes\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1542.001 System Firmware\n- T1014 Rootkit\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0348": "# S0348 Cardinal RAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0348\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0348\n\n[Cardinal RAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0348) is a potentially low volume remote access trojan (RAT) observed since December 2015. [Cardinal RAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0348) is notable for its unique utilization of uncompiled C# source code and the Microsoft Windows built-in csc.exe compiler.(Citation: PaloAlto CardinalRat Apr 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1083 File and Directory Discover