vice\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1110": "# S1110 SLIGHTPULSE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1110\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1110\n\n[SLIGHTPULSE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1110) is a web shell that was used by [APT5](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1023) as early as 2020 including against Pulse Secure VPNs at US Defense Industrial Base (DIB) entities.(Citation: Mandiant Pulse Secure Zero-Day April 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1059 Command and Scripting Interpreter\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0272": "# S0272 NDiskMonitor\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0272\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0272\n\n[NDiskMonitor](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0272) is a custom backdoor written in .NET that appears to be unique to [Patchwork](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0040). (Citation: TrendMicro Patchwork Dec 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0369": "# S0369 CoinTicker\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0369\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0369\n\n[CoinTicker](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0369) is a malicious application that poses as a cryptocurrency price ticker and installs components of the open source backdoors EvilOSX and EggShell.(Citation: CoinTicker 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1543.001 Launch Agent\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1553.001 Gatekeeper Bypass\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0255": "# S0255 DDKONG\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0255\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0255\n\n[DDKONG](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0255) is a malware sample that was part of a campaign by [Rancor](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0075). [DDKONG](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0255) was first seen used in February 2017. (Citation: Rancor Unit42 June 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0587": "# S0587 Penquin\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0587\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0587\n\n[Penquin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0587) is a remote access trojan (RAT) with multiple versions used by [Turla](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0010) to target Linux systems since at least 2014.(Citation: Kaspersky Turla Penquin December 2014)(Citation: Leonardo Turla Penquin May 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1205 Traffic Signaling\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1053.003 Cron\n- T1040 Network Sniffing\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1027.005 Indicator Removal from Tools\n- T1222.002 Linux and Mac Permissions\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1205.002 Socket Filters\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9024": "# S9024 SPAWNCHIMERA\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9024\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9024\n\n[SPAWNCHIMERA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9024) is a backdoor that supports command and control and can inject malicious components into native processes.(Citation: CISA SPAWNCHIMERA RESURGE February 2026)(Citation: Google UNC5221 BRICKSTORM SPAWNCHIMERA April 2024)(Citation: JPCERT SPAWNCHIMERA Ivanti February 2025)  [SPAWNCHIMERA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9024) It incorporates capabilities from multiple tools within the SPAWN malware family, including SPAWNANT, SPAWNMOLE, and SPAWNSNAIL.(Citation: Google UNC5221 Ivanti January 2025)(Citation: Google UNC5221 BRICKSTORM SPAWNCHIMERA April 2024)(Citation: JPCERT SPAWNCHIMERA Ivanti February 2025)  [SPAWNCHIMERA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9024) was first reported in April 2024.(Citation: Google UNC5221 BRICKSTORM SPAWNCHIMERA April 2024) [SPAWNCHIMERA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9024) has been observed in activity attributed to People's Republic of China (PRC) state-sponsored threat actors, including UNC5221..(Citation: Google UNC5221 Ivanti January 2025)(Citation: Google UNC5221 Ivanti April 2025)(Citation: Google UNC5221 BRICKSTORM SPAWNCHIMERA April 2024)(Citation: Picus Security UNC5221 Ivanti May 2025)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1678 Delay Execution\n- T1505.003 Web Shell\n- T1574 Hijack Execution Flow\n- T1037 Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1572 Protocol Tunneling\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1574.006 Dynamic Linker Hijacking\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1055.002 Portable Executable Injection\n- T1559 Inter-Process Communication\n- T1040 Network Sniffing\n- T1690 Prevent Command History Logging\n- T1571 Non-Standard Port\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1480.002 Mutual Exclusion\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0414": "# S0414 BabyShark\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0414\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0414\n\n[BabyShark](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0414) is a Microsoft Visual Basic (VB) script-based malware family that is believed to be associated with several North Korean campaigns. (Citation: Unit42 BabyShark Feb 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1218.005 Mshta\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0351": "# S0351 Cannon\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0351\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0351\n\n[Cannon](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0351) is a Trojan with variants written in C# and Delphi. It was first observed in April 2018. (Citation: Unit42 Cannon Nov 2018)(Citation: Unit42 Sofacy Dec 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1071.003 Mail Protocols\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1547.004 Winlogon Helper DLL\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1024": "# S1024 CreepySnail\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1024\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1024\n\n[CreepySnail](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1024) is a custom PowerShell implant that has been used by [POLONIUM](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1005) since at least 2022.(Citation: Microsoft POLONIUM June 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1078.002 Domain Accounts\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0471": "# S0471 build_downer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0471\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0471\n\n[build_downer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0471) is a downloader that has been used by [BRONZE BUTLER](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0060) since at least 2019.(Citation: Trend Micro Tick November 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1027.003 Steganography\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0530": "# S0530 Melcoz\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0530\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0530\n\n[Melcoz](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0530) is a banking trojan family built from the open source tool Remote Access PC. [Melcoz](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0530) was first observed in attacks in Brazil and since 2018 has spread to Chile, Mexico, Spain, and Portugal.(Citation: Securelist Brazilian Banking Malware July 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1565.002 Transmitted Data Manipulation\n- T1059.010 AutoHotKey & AutoIT\n- T1218.007 Msiexec\n- T1185 Browser Session Hijacking\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1204.001 Malicious Link\n- T1566.002 Spearphishing Link\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0141": "# S0141 Winnti for Windows\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0141\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0141\n\n[Winnti for Windows](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0141) is a modular remote access Trojan (RAT) that has been used likely by multiple groups to carry out intrusions in various regions since at least 2010, including by one group referred to as the same name, [Winnti Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0044).(Citation: Kaspersky Winnti April 2013)(Citation: Microsoft Winnti Jan 2017)(Citation: Novetta Winnti April 2015)(Citation: 401 TRG Winnti Umbrella May 2018). The Linux variant is tracked separately under [Winnti for Linux](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0430).(Citation: Chronicle Winnti for Linux May 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1090.002 External Proxy\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1090.001 Internal Proxy\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1027.015 Compression\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1480.001 Environmental Keying\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0685": "# S0685 PowerPunch\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0685\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0685\n\n[PowerPunch](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0685) is a lightweight downloader that has been used by [Gamaredon Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0047) since at least 2021.(Citation: Microsoft Actinium February 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1480.001 Environmental Keying\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0360": "# S0360 BONDUPDATER\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0360\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0360\n\n[BONDUPDATER](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0360) is a PowerShell backdoor used by [OilRig](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0049). It was first observed in November 2017 during targeting of a Middle Eastern government organization, and an updated version was observed in August 2018 being used to target a government organization with spearphishing emails.(Citation: FireEye APT34 Dec 2017)(Citation: Palo Alto OilRig Sep 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1564.003 Hidden Window\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1568.002 Domain Generation Algorithms\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1196": "# S1196 Troll Stealer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1196\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1196\n\n[Troll Stealer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1196) is an information stealer written in Go associated with [Kimsuky](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0094) operations. [Troll Stealer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1196) has typically been delivered through a dropper disguised as a legitimate security program installation file. [Troll Stealer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1196) features code similar to [AppleSeed](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0622), also uniquely associated with [Kimsuky](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0094) operations.(Citation: S2W Troll Stealer 2024)(Citation: Symantec Troll Stealer 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1552.004 Private Keys\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1213 Data from Information Repositories\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1480.002 Mutual Exclusion\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1217 Browser Information Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0069": "# S0069 BLACKCOFFEE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0069\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0069\n\n[BLACKCOFFEE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0069) is malware that has been used by several Chinese groups since at least 2013. (Citation: FireEye APT17) (Citation: FireEye Periscope March 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1104 Multi-Stage Channels\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1102.001 Dead Drop Resolver\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1136": "# S1136 BFG Agonizer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1136\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1136\n\n[BFG Agonizer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1136) is a wiper related to the open-source project CRYLINE-v.5.0. The malware is associated with wiping operations conducted by the [Agrius](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1030) threat actor.(Citation: Unit42 Agrius 2023)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1554 Compromise Host Software Binary\n- T1561.002 Disk Structure Wipe\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0377": "# S0377 Ebury\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0377\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0377\n\n[Ebury](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0377) is an OpenSSH backdoor and credential stealer targeting Linux servers and container hosts developed by [Windigo](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0124). [Ebury](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0377) is primarily installed through modifying shared libraries (`.so` files) executed by the legitimate OpenSSH program. First seen in 2009, [Ebury](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0377) has been used to maintain a botnet of servers, deploy additional malware, and steal cryptocurrency wallets, credentials, and credit card details.(Citation: ESET Ebury Feb 2014)(Citation: BleepingComputer Ebury March 2017)(Citation: ESET Ebury Oct 2017)(Citation: ESET Ebury May 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1014 Rootkit\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1556.003 Pluggable Authentication Modules\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1556 Modify Authentication Process\n- T1020 Automated Exfiltration\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1685.004 Disable or Modify Linux Audit System Log\n- T1554 Compromise Host Software Binary\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1574.006 Dynamic Linker Hijacking\n- T1129 Shared Modules\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1552.004 Private Keys\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1568.002 Domain Generation Algorithms\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0599": "# S0599 Kinsing\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0599\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0599\n\n[Kinsing](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0599) is Golang-based malware that runs a cryptocurrency miner and attempts to spread itself to other hosts in the victim environment. (Citation: Aqua Kinsing April 2020)(Citation: Sysdig Kinsing November 2020)(Citation: Aqua Security Cloud Native Threat Report June 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1610 Deploy Container\n- T1078 Valid Accounts\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1552.004 Private Keys\n- T1222.002 Linux and Mac Permissions\n- T1552.003 Shell History\n- T1110 Brute Force\n- T1021.004 SSH\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1133 External Remote Services\n- T1053.003 Cron\n- T1609 Container Administration Command\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1496.001 Compute Hijacking\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1123": "# S1123 PITSTOP\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1123\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1123\n\n[PITSTOP](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1123) is a backdoor that was deployed on compromised Ivanti Connect Secure VPNs during [Cutting Edge](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0029) to enable command execution and file read/write.(Citation: Mandiant Cutting Edge Part 3 February 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1559 Inter-Process Communication\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1205.002 Socket Filters\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0688": "# S0688 Meteor\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0688\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0688\n\n[Meteor](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0688) is a wiper that was used against Iranian government organizations, including Iranian Railways, the Ministry of Roads, and Urban Development systems, in July 2021. [Meteor](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0688) is likely a newer version of similar wipers called Stardust and Comet that were reportedly used by a group called \"Indra\" since at least 2019 against private companies in Syria.(Citation: Check Point Meteor Aug 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1491.001 Internal Defacement\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1531 Account Access Removal\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1564.003 Hidden Window\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1484.001 Group Policy Modification\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0385": "# S0385 njRAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0385\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0385\n\n[njRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0385) is a remote access tool (RAT) that was first observed in 2012. It has been used by threat actors in the Middle East.(Citation: Fidelis njRAT June 2013)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1010 Application Window Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1027.004 Compile After Delivery\n- T1571 Non-Standard Port\n- T1091 Replication Through Removable Media\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1021.001 Remote Desktop Protocol\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1686.003 Windows Host Firewall\n- T1070.009 Clear Persistence\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1568.001 Fast Flux DNS\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1114": "# S1114 ZIPLINE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1114\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1114\n\n[ZIPLINE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1114) is a passive backdoor that was used during [Cutting Edge](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0029) on compromised Secure Connect VPNs for reverse shell and proxy functionality.(Citation: Mandiant Cutting Edge January 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1205 Traffic Signaling\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0449": "# S0449 Maze\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0449\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0449\n\n[Maze](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0449) ransomware, previously known as \"ChaCha\", was discovered in May 2019. In addition to encrypting files on victim machines for impact, [Maze](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0449) operators conduct information stealing campaigns prior to encryption and post the information online to extort affected companies.(Citation: FireEye Maze May 2020)(Citation: McAfee Maze March 2020)(Citation: Sophos Maze VM September 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1027.016 Junk Code Insertion\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1614.001 System Language Discovery\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1564.006 Run Virtual Instance\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1568 Dynamic Resolution\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1070 Indicator Removal\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1218.007 Msiexec\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0114": "# S0114 BOOTRASH\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0114\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0114\n\n[BOOTRASH](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0114) is a [Bootkit](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1542/003) that targets Windows operating systems. It has been used by threat actors that target the financial sector.(Citation: Mandiant M Trends 2016)(Citation: FireEye Bootkits)(Citation: FireEye BOOTRASH SANS)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1564.005 Hidden File System\n- T1542.003 Bootkit\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1230": "# S1230 HIUPAN\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1230\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1230\n\n[HIUPAN](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1230) (aka U2DiskWatch) is a is a worm that propagates through removable drives known to be leveraged by [Mustang Panda](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0129) and was first observed utilized in 2024. (Citation: 2025_IBM_PUBLOAD_TONESHELL_HIUPAN_CLAIMLOADER_MUSTANG PANDA)(Citation: Trend Micro MUSTANG PANDA PUBLOAD HIUPAN SEPTEMBER 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1091 Replication Through Removable Media\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1678 Delay Execution\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0126": "# S0126 ComRAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0126\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0126\n\n[ComRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0126) is a second stage implant suspected of being a descendant of [Agent.btz](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0092) and used by [Turla](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0010). The first version of [ComRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0126) was identified in 2007, but the tool has undergone substantial development for many years since.(Citation: Symantec Waterbug)(Citation: NorthSec 2015 GData Uroburos Tools)(Citation: ESET ComRAT May 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.003 Mail Protocols\n- T1564.005 Hidden File System\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1027.011 Fileless Storage\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1029 Scheduled Transfer\n- T1546.015 Component Object Model Hijacking\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1027.009 Embedded Payloads\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0199": "# S0199 TURNEDUP\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0199\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0199\n\n[TURNEDUP](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0199) is a non-public backdoor. It has been dropped by [APT33](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0064)'s [StoneDrill](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0380) malware. (Citation: FireEye APT33 Sept 2017) (Citation: FireEye APT33 Webinar Sept 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1055.004 Asynchronous Procedure Call\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0144": "# S0144 ChChes\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0144\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0144\n\n[ChChes](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0144) is a Trojan that appears to be used exclusively by [menuPass](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0045). It was used to target Japanese organizations in 2016. Its lack of persistence methods suggests it may be intended as a first-stage tool. (Citation: Palo Alto menuPass Feb 2017) (Citation: JPCERT ChChes Feb 2017) (Citation: PWC Cloud Hopper Technical Annex April 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0393": "# S0393 PowerStallion\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0393\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0393\n\n[PowerStallion](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0393) is a lightweight [PowerShell](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1059/001) backdoor used by [Turla](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0010), possibly as a recovery access tool to install other backdoors.(Citation: ESET Turla PowerShell May 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1074": "# S1074 ANDROMEDA\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1074\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1074\n\n[ANDROMEDA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1074) is commodity malware that was widespread in the early 2010's and continues to be observed in infections across a wide variety of industries. During the 2022 [C0026](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0026) campaign, threat actors re-registered expired [ANDROMEDA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1074) C2 domains to spread malware to select targets in Ukraine.(Citation: Mandiant Suspected Turla Campaign February 2023)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1036.008 Masquerade File Type\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1091 Replication Through Removable Media\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1156": "# S1156 Manjusaka\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1156\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1156\n\n[Manjusaka](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1156) is a Chinese-language intrusion framework, similar to [Sliver](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0633) and [Cobalt Strike](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0154), with an ELF binary written in GoLang as the controller for Windows and Linux implants written in Rust. First identified in 2022, [Manjusaka](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1156) consists of multiple components, only one of which (a command and control module) is freely available.(Citation: Talos Manjusaka 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1555 Credentials from Password Stores\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1022": "# S1022 IceApple\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1022\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1022\n\n[IceApple](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1022) is a modular Internet Information Services (IIS) post-exploitation framework, that has been used since at least 2021 against the technology, academic, and government sectors.(Citation: CrowdStrike IceApple May 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1003.004 LSA Secrets\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1552.002 Credentials in Registry\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1505.004 IIS Components\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1620 Reflective Code Loading\n- T1056.003 Web Portal Capture\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1087.002 Domain Account\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1003.002 Security Account Manager\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9008": "# S9008 Shai-Hulud\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9008\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9008\n\n[Shai-Hulud](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9008) is a supply chain worm, first reported in September 2025, that spreads through code repositories, including GitHub and NPM packages. It exploits CI/CD pipeline dependencies to propagate to victims and poisons the supply chain by publishing malicious packages. Once inside a victim environment, [Shai-Hulud](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9008) steals credentials and access tokens from compromised repository accounts and exfiltrates them to attacker-controlled servers via encoded GitHub Actions workflows.(Citation: Palo Alto Unit 42 Shai-Hulud November 2025)(Citation: Microsoft Shai-Hulud December 2025)(Citation: Socket Shai-Hulud November 2025)(Citation: Socket Shai-Hulud Trufflehog September 2025)(Citation: Aikido Shai-Hulud September 2025)(Citation: Netskope Shai-Hulud November 2025)(Citation: Wiz Shai-Hulud September 2025)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1548.003 Sudo and Sudo Caching\n- T1543.002 Systemd Service\n- T1678 Delay Execution\n- T1677 Poisoned Pipeline Execution\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n- T1552.001 Credentials In Files\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1195.001 Compromise Software Dependencies and Development Tools\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1078.004 Cloud Accounts\n- T1555.006 Cloud Secrets Management Stores\n- T1564.011 Ignore Process Interrupts\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1593.003 Code Repositories\n- T1567.001 Exfiltration to Code Repository\n- T1036.009 Break Process Trees\n- T1528 Steal Application Access Token\n- T1098 Account Manipulation\n- T1553 Subvert Trust Controls\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1546.016 Installer Packages\n- T1608.001 Upload Malware\n- T1567.004 Exfiltration Over Webhook\n- T1213.003 Code Repositories\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1552.005 Cloud Instance Metadata API\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1550.001 Application Access Token\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0201": "# S0201 JPIN\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0201\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0201\n\n[JPIN](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0201) is a custom-built backdoor family used by [PLATINUM](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0068). Evidence suggests developers of [JPIN](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0201) and [Dipsind](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0200) code bases were related in some way. (Citation: Microsoft PLATINUM April 2016)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1222.001 Windows Permissions\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1069.001 Local Groups\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1197 BITS Jobs\n- T1071.003 Mail Protocols\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1071.002 File Transfer Protocols\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1217": "# S1217 VIRTUALPITA\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1217\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1217\n\n[VIRTUALPITA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1217) is a passive backdoor with ESXi and Linux vCenter variants capable of command execution, file transfer, and starting and stopping processes. [VIRTUALPITA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1217) has been in use since at least 2022 including by [UNC3886](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1048) who leveraged malicious vSphere Installation Bundles (VIBs) for install on ESXi hypervisors.(Citation: Google Cloud Threat Intelligence ESXi VIBs 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1675 ESXi Administration Command\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1673 Virtual Machine Discovery\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1037 Boot or Logon Initialization Scripts\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer\n- T1690 Prevent Command History Logging\n- T1571 Non-Standard Port\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1059": "# S1059 metaMain\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1059\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1059\n\n[metaMain](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1059) is a backdoor used by [Metador](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1013) to maintain long-term access to compromised machines; it has also been used to decrypt [Mafalda](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1060) into memory.(Citation: SentinelLabs Metador Sept 2022)(Citation: SentinelLabs Metador Technical Appendix Sept 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1546.003 Windows Management Instrumentation Event Subscription\n- T1620 Reflective Code Loading\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1560.003 Archive via Custom Method\n- T1056 Input Capture\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1090.001 Internal Proxy\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1205.001 Port Knocking\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0610": "# S0610 SideTwist\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0610\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0610\n\n[SideTwist](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0610) is a C-based backdoor that has been used by [OilRig](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0049) since at least 2021.(Citation: Check Point APT34 April 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1001 Data Obfuscation\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0669": "# S0669 KOCTOPUS\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0669\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0669\n\n[KOCTOPUS](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0669)'s batch variant is loader used by [LazyScripter](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0140) since 2018 to launch [Octopus](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0340) and [Koadic](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0250) and, in some cases, [QuasarRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0262). [KOCTOPUS](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0669) also has a VBA variant that has the same functionality as the batch version.(Citation: MalwareBytes LazyScripter Feb 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1566.002 Spearphishing Link\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1564.003 Hidden Window\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1204.001 Malicious Link\n- T1070.009 Clear Persistence\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0459": "# S0459 MechaFlounder\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0459\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0459\n\n[MechaFlounder](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0459) is a python-based remote access tool (RAT) that has been used by [APT39](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0087). The payload uses a combination of actor developed code and code snippets freely available online in development communities.(Citation: Unit 42 MechaFlounder March 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0078": "# S0078 Psylo\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0078\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0078\n\n[Psylo](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0078) is a shellcode-based Trojan that has been used by [Scarlet Mimic](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0029). It has similar characteristics as [FakeM](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0076). (Citation: Scarlet Mimic Jan 2016)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1027": "# S1027 Heyoka Backdoor\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1027\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1027\n\n[Heyoka Backdoor](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1027) is a custom backdoor--based on the Heyoka open source exfiltration tool--that  has been used by [Aoqin Dragon](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1007) since at least 2013.(Citation: SentinelOne Aoqin Dragon June 2022)(Citation: Sourceforge Heyoka 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1572 Protocol Tunneling\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0070": "# S0070 HTTPBrowser\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0070\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0070\n\n[HTTPBrowser](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0070) is malware that has been used by several threat groups. (Citation: ThreatStream Evasion Analysis) (Citation: Dell TG-3390) It is believed to be of Chinese origin. (Citation: ThreatConnect Anthem)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9011": "# S9011 BRUSHFIRE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9011\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9011\n\n[BRUSHFIRE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9011) is a passive backdoor written in C that executes in-memory within an existing process. First reported in March 2025, [BRUSHFIRE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9011) has been observed in activity attributed to People's Republic of China (PRC) state-affiliated threat actors, including UNC5221 and SYLVANITE.(Citation: Dragos SYLVANITE MuddyWater Electrum March 2026)(Citation: Google UNC5221 Ivanti April 2025)(Citation: Picus Security UNC5221 Ivanti May 2025)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1048.002 Exfiltration Over Asymmetric Encrypted Non-C2 Protocol\n- T1205 Traffic Signaling\n- T1620 Reflective Code Loading\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0084": "# S0084 Mis-Type\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0084\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0084\n\n[Mis-Type](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0084) is a backdoor hybrid that was used in [Operation Dust Storm](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0016) by 2012.(Citation: Cylance Dust Storm)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1547 Boot or Logon Autostart Execution\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1136.001 Local Account\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1008 Fallback Channels\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1141": "# S1141 LunarWeb\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1141\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1141\n\n[LunarWeb](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1141) is a backdoor that has been used by [Turla](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0010) since at least 2020 including in a compromise of a European ministry of foreign affairs (MFA) together with [LunarLoader](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1143) and [LunarMail](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1142). [LunarWeb](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1141) has only been observed deployed against servers and can use [Steganography](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1001/002) to obfuscate command and control.(Citation: ESET Turla Lunar toolset May 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1615 Group Policy Discovery\n- T1001.002 Steganography\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1104 Multi-Stage Channels\n- T1560.002 Archive via Library\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1572 Protocol Tunneling\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1069.001 Local Groups\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1030 Data Transfer Size Limits\n- T1559 Inter-Process Communication\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0658": "# S0658 XCSSET\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0658\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0658\n\n[XCSSET](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0658) is a modular macOS malware family delivered through infected Xcode projects and executed when the project is compiled. Active since August 2020, it has been observed installing backdoors, spoofed browsers, collecting data, and encrypting user files. It is composed of SHC-compiled shell scripts and run-only AppleScripts, often hiding in apps that mimic system tools (such as Xcode, Mail, or Notes) or use familiar icons (like Launchpad) to avoid detection.(Citation: trendmicro xcsset xcode project 2020)(Citation: April 2021 TrendMicro XCSSET)(Citation: Microsoft March 2025 XCSSET)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1614.001 System Language Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1553.001 Gatekeeper Bypass\n- T1036 Masquerading\n- T1195.001 Compromise Software Dependencies and Development Tools\n- T1098.004 SSH Authorized Keys\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1574.006 Dynamic Linker Hijacking\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1548.006 TCC Manipulation\n- T1222.002 Linux and Mac Permissions\n- T1539 Steal Web Session Cookie\n- T1056.002 GUI Input Capture\n- T1068 Exploitation for Privilege Escalation\n- T1569.001 Launchctl\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1543.004 Launch Daemon\n- T1647 Plist File Modification\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1554 Compromise Host Software Binary\n- T1087 Account Discovery\n- T1546 Event Triggered Execution\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1546.004 Unix Shell Configuration Modification\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1088": "# S1088 Disco\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1088\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1088\n\n[Disco](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1088) is a custom implant that has been used by [MoustachedBouncer](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1019) since at least 2020 including in campaigns using targeted malicious content injection for initial access and command and control.(Citation: MoustachedBouncer ESET August 2023)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1659 Content Injection\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1071.002 File Transfer Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0200": "# S0200 Dipsind\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0200\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0200\n\n[Dipsind](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0200) is a malware family of backdoors that appear to be used exclusively by [PLATINUM](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0068). (Citation: Microsoft PLATINUM April 2016)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1547.004 Winlogon Helper DLL\n- T1029 Scheduled Transfer\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0340": "# S0340 Octopus\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0340\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0340\n\n[Octopus](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0340) is a Windows Trojan written in the Delphi programming language that has been used by [Nomadic Octopus](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0133) to target government organizations in Central Asia since at least 2014.(Citation: Securelist Octopus Oct 2018)(Citation: Security Affairs DustSquad Oct 2018)(Citation: ESET Nomadic Octopus 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1567.002 Exfiltration to Cloud Storage\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0607": "# S0607 KillDisk\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0607\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0607\n\n[KillDisk](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0607) is a disk-wiping tool designed to overwrite files with random data to render the OS unbootable. It was first observed as a component of [BlackEnergy](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0089) malware during cyber attacks against Ukraine in 2015. [KillDisk](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0607) has since evolved into stand-alone malware used by a variety of threat actors against additional targets in Europe and Latin America; in 2016 a ransomware component was also incorporated into some [KillDisk](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0607) variants.(Citation: KillDisk Ransomware)(Citation: ESEST Black Energy Jan 2016)(Citation: Trend Micro KillDisk 1)(Citation: Trend Micro KillDisk 2)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1129 Shared Modules\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1561.002 Disk Structure Wipe\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1134 Access Token Manipulation\n- T1485 Data Destruction\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1242": "# S1242 Qilin\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1242\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1242\n\n[Qilin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1242) is a ransomware family operated as a ransomware-as-a-service (RaaS) that has been active since at least 2022. It includes variants written in Go and Rust capable of targeting Windows, Linux, and VMware ESXi environments. [Qilin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1242) shares functionality overlaps with [Black Basta](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1070), [REvil](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0496), and [BlackCat](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1068) ransomware. [Qilin](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1242) affiliates have targeted multiple entities worldwide with the majority of victims in the US, France, Canada, and the UK, primarily in the manufacturing, technology, financial services, and healthcare sectors.(Citation: Trend Micro Agenda Ransomware AUG 2022)(Citation: SentinelOne Qilin NOV 2022)(Citation: BushidoToken Qilin RaaS JUN 2024)(Citation: Sophos Qilin MSP APR 2025)(Citation: Trend Micro Agenda Ransomware OCT 2025)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1134 Access Token Manipulation\n- T1480.002 Mutual Exclusion\n- T1547.004 Winlogon Helper DLL\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1071.002 File Transfer Protocols\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1566.002 Spearphishing Link\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1673 Virtual Machine Discovery\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1069.002 Domain Groups\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1222 File and Directory Permissions Modification\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1204.001 Malicious Link\n- T1190 Exploit Public-Facing Application\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1484.001 Group Policy Modification\n- T1021.004 SSH\n- T1003.001 LSASS Memory\n- T1678 Delay Execution\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1688 Safe Mode Boot\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1087.002 Domain Account\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1491.001 Internal Defacement\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1219.002 Remote Desktop Software\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0584": "# S0584 AppleJeus\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0584\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0584\n\n[AppleJeus](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0584) is a family of downloaders initially discovered in 2018 embedded within trojanized cryptocurrency applications. [AppleJeus](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0584) has been used by [Lazarus Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0032), targeting companies in the energy, finance, government, industry, technology, and telecommunications sectors, and several countries including the United States, United Kingdom, South Korea, Australia, Brazil, New Zealand, and Russia. [AppleJeus](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0584) has been used to distribute the [FALLCHILL](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0181) RAT.(Citation: CISA AppleJeus Feb 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1546.016 Installer Packages\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1566.002 Spearphishing Link\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1569.001 Launchctl\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1218.007 Msiexe