 [jRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0283) have been distributed via a software-as-a-service platform, similar to an online subscription model.(Citation: Kaspersky Adwind Feb 2016) (Citation: jRAT Symantec Aug 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1029 Scheduled Transfer\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1037.005 Startup Items\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1021.001 Remote Desktop Protocol\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1552.001 Credentials In Files\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1552.004 Private Keys\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0170": "# S0170 Helminth\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0170\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0170\n\n[Helminth](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0170) is a backdoor that has at least two variants - one written in VBScript and PowerShell that is delivered via a macros in Excel spreadsheets, and one that is a standalone Windows executable. (Citation: Palo Alto OilRig May 2016)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1547.009 Shortcut Modification\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n