rbon Spider August 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0158": "# S0158 PHOREAL\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0158\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0158\n\n[PHOREAL](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0158) is a signature backdoor used by [APT32](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0050). (Citation: FireEye APT32 May 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0165": "# S0165 OSInfo\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0165\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0165\n\n[OSInfo](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0165) is a custom tool used by [APT3](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0022) to do internal discovery on a victim's computer and network. (Citation: Symantec Buckeye)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1069.002 Domain Groups\n- T1087.002 Domain Account\n- T1069.001 Local Groups\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0282": "# S0282 MacSpy\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0282\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0282\n\n[MacSpy](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0282) is a malware-as-a-service offered on the darkweb  (Citation: objsee mac malware 2017).\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1090.003 Multi-hop Proxy\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1543.001 Launch Agent\n- T1115 Clipboard Data\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0681": "# S0681 Lizar\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0681\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0681\n\n[Lizar](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0681) is a modular remote access tool written using the .NET Framework that shares structural similarities to [Carbanak](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0030). It has likely been used by [FIN7](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0046) since at least February 2021.(Citation: BiZone Lizar May 2021)(Citation: Threatpost Lizar May 2021)(Citation: Gemini FIN7 Oct 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1217 Browser Information Discovery\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1055.001 Dynamic-link Library Injection\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1003.001 LSASS Memory\n- T1555.004 Windows Credential Manager\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1055.002 Portable Executable Injection\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1087.003 Email Account\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1573 Encrypted Channel\n- T1620 Reflective Code Loading\n- T1588.002 Tool\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1132.002 Non-Standard Encoding\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0567": "# S0567 Dtrack\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0567\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0567\n\n[Dtrack](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0567) is spyware that was discovered in 2019 and has been used against Indian financial institutions, research facilities, and the Kudankulam Nuclear Power Plant. [Dtrack](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0567) shares similarities with the DarkSeoul campaign, which was attributed to [Lazarus Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0032). (Citation: Kaspersky Dtrack)(Citation: Securelist Dtrack)(Citation: Dragos WASSONITE)(Citation: CyberBit Dtrack)(Citation: ZDNet Dtrack)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1574 Hijack Execution Flow\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1547 Boot or Logon Autostart Execution\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1078 Valid Accounts\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1217 Browser Information Discovery\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1027.009 Embedded Payloads\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1129 Shared Modules\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0132": "# S0132 H1N1\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0132\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0132\n\n[H1N1](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0132) is a malware variant that has been distributed via a campaign using VBA macros to infect victims. Although it initially had only loader capabilities, it has evolved to include information-stealing functionality. (Citation: Cisco H1N1 Part 1)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1080 Taint Shared Content\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1091 Replication Through Removable Media\n- T1686.003 Windows Host Firewall\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1132 Data Encoding\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1104": "# S1104 SLOWPULSE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1104\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1104\n\n[SLOWPULSE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1104) is a malware that was used by [APT5](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1023) as early as 2020 including against U.S. Defense Industrial Base (DIB) companies. [SLOWPULSE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1104) has several variants and can modify legitimate Pulse Secure VPN files in order to log credentials and bypass single and two-factor authentication flows.(Citation: Mandiant Pulse Secure Zero-Day April 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1556.004 Network Device Authentication\n- T1111 Multi-Factor Authentication Interception\n- T1556.006 Multi-Factor Authentication\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1554 Compromise Host Software Binary\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0639": "# S0639 Seth-Locker\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0639\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0639\n\n[Seth-Locker](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0639) is a ransomware with some remote control capabilities that has been in use since at least 2021.\n(Citation: Trend Micro Ransomware February 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0451": "# S0451 LoudMiner\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0451\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0451\n\n[LoudMiner](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0451) is a cryptocurrency miner which uses virtualization software to siphon system resources. The miner has been bundled with pirated copies of Virtual Studio Technology (VST) for Windows and macOS.(Citation: ESET LoudMiner June 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1569.001 Launchctl\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1059.004 Unix Shell\n- T1543.004 Launch Daemon\n- T1496.001 Compute Hijacking\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1189 Drive-by Compromise\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1564.006 Run Virtual Instance\n- T1218.007 Msiexec\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0344": "# S0344 Azorult\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0344\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0344\n\n[Azorult](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0344) is a commercial Trojan that is used to steal information from compromised hosts. [Azorult](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0344) has been observed in the wild as early as 2016.\nIn July 2018, [Azorult](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0344) was seen used in a spearphishing campaign against targets in North America. [Azorult](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0344) has been seen used for cryptocurrency theft. (Citation: Unit42 Azorult Nov 2018)(Citation: Proofpoint Azorult July 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1134.002 Create Process with Token\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1552.001 Credentials In Files\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0570": "# S0570 BitPaymer\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0570\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0570\n\n[BitPaymer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0570) is a ransomware variant first observed in August 2017 targeting hospitals in the U.K. [BitPaymer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0570) uses a unique encryption key, ransom note, and contact information for each operation. [BitPaymer](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0570) has several indicators suggesting overlap with the [Dridex](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0384) malware and is often delivered via [Dridex](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0384).(Citation: Crowdstrike Indrik November 2018)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1222.001 Windows Permissions\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1134.001 Token Impersonation/Theft\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1564.004 NTFS File Attributes\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1106 Native API\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0031": "# S0031 BACKSPACE\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0031\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0031\n\n[BACKSPACE](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0031) is a backdoor used by [APT30](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0013) that dates back to at least 2005. (Citation: FireEye APT30)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1132.002 Non-Standard Encoding\n- T1090.001 Internal Proxy\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1104 Multi-Stage Channels\n- T1547.009 Shortcut Modification\n- T1686 Disable or Modify System Firewall\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0672": "# S0672 Zox\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0672\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0672\n\n[Zox](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0672) is a remote access tool that has been used by [Axiom](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0001) since at least 2008.(Citation: Novetta-Axiom)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1001.002 Steganography\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1068 Exploitation for Privilege Escalation\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0275": "# S0275 UPPERCUT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0275\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0275\n\n[UPPERCUT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0275) is a 32-bit HTTP-based backdoor that has been used by [menuPass](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0045) since at least 2017.(Citation: FireEye APT10 Sept 2018) Once thought to be exclusive to [menuPass](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0045), [UPPERCUT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0275) was also observed being used by [menuPass](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0045)-associated [MirrorFace](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1054) during [Operation AkaiRyū](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0060).(Citation: Trend Micro Earth Kasha Anel NOV 2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1678 Delay Execution\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0045": "# S0045 ADVSTORESHELL\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0045\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0045\n\n[ADVSTORESHELL](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0045) is a spying backdoor that has been used by [APT28](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0007) from at least 2012 to 2016. It is generally used for long-term espionage and is deployed on targets deemed interesting after a reconnaissance phase. (Citation: Kaspersky Sofacy) (Citation: ESET Sednit Part 2)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1546.015 Component Object Model Hijacking\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1029 Scheduled Transfer\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1012 Query Registry\n- T1120 Peripheral Device Discovery\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1560.003 Archive via Custom Method\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1034": "# S1034 StrifeWater\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1034\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1034\n\n[StrifeWater](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1034) is a remote-access tool that has been used by [Moses Staff](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1009) in the initial stages of their attacks since at least November 2021.(Citation: Cybereason StrifeWater Feb 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0080": "# S0080 Mivast\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0080\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0080\n\n[Mivast](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0080) is a backdoor that has been used by [Deep Panda](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0009). It was reportedly used in the Anthem breach. (Citation: Symantec Black Vine)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1003.002 Security Account Manager\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0394": "# S0394 HiddenWasp\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0394\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0394\n\n[HiddenWasp](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0394) is a Linux-based Trojan used to target systems for remote control. It comes in the form of a statically linked ELF binary with stdlibc++.(Citation: Intezer HiddenWasp Map 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1136.001 Local Account\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1574.006 Dynamic Linker Hijacking\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1037.004 RC Scripts\n- T1014 Rootkit\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0670": "# S0670 WarzoneRAT\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0670\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0670\n\n[WarzoneRAT](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0670) is a malware-as-a-service remote access tool (RAT) written in C++ that has been publicly available for purchase since at least late 2018.(Citation: Check Point Warzone Feb 2020)(Citation: Uptycs Warzone UAC Bypass November 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1204.002 Malicious File\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1021.001 Remote Desktop Protocol\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1546.015 Component Object Model Hijacking\n- T1564 Hide Artifacts\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1125 Video Capture\n- T1564.003 Hidden Window\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1021.005 VNC\n- T1221 Template Injection\n- T1095 Non-Application Layer Protocol\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1014 Rootkit\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0056": "# S0056 Net Crawler\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0056\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0056\n\n[Net Crawler](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0056) is an intranet worm capable of extracting credentials using credential dumpers and spreading to systems on a network over SMB by brute forcing accounts with recovered passwords and using [PsExec](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0029) to execute a copy of [Net Crawler](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0056). (Citation: Cylance Cleaver)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1110.002 Password Cracking\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1003.001 LSASS Memory\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0533": "# S0533 SLOTHFULMEDIA\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0533\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0533\n\n[SLOTHFULMEDIA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0533) is a remote access Trojan written in C++ that has been used by an unidentified \"sophisticated cyber actor\" since at least January 2017.(Citation: CISA MAR SLOTHFULMEDIA October 2020)(Citation: Costin Raiu IAmTheKing October 2020) It has been used to target government organizations, defense contractors, universities, and energy companies in Russia, India, Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Malaysia, Ukraine, and Eastern Europe.(Citation: USCYBERCOM SLOTHFULMEDIA October 2020)(Citation: Kaspersky IAmTheKing October 2020)  \n\nIn October 2020, Kaspersky Labs assessed [SLOTHFULMEDIA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0533) is part of an activity cluster it refers to as \"IAmTheKing\".(Citation: Kaspersky IAmTheKing October 2020) ESET also noted code similarity between [SLOTHFULMEDIA](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0533) and droppers used by a group it refers to as \"PowerPool\".(Citation: ESET PowerPool Code October 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1001 Data Obfuscation\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0181": "# S0181 FALLCHILL\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0181\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0181\n\n[FALLCHILL](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0181) is a RAT that has been used by [Lazarus Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0032) since at least 2016 to target the aerospace, telecommunications, and finance industries. It is usually dropped by other [Lazarus Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0032) malware or delivered when a victim unknowingly visits a compromised website. (Citation: US-CERT FALLCHILL Nov 2017)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1070.006 Timestomp\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1001.003 Protocol or Service Impersonation\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1248": "# S1248 XORIndex Loader\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1248\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1248\n\n[XORIndex Loader](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1248) is a XOR-encoded loader that collects host data, decodes follow-on scripts and acts as a downloader for the [BeaverTail](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1246) malware.  [XORIndex Loader](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1248) was first reported in June 2025.  [XORIndex Loader](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1248) has been leveraged by North Korea-affiliated threat actors identified as [Contagious Interview](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1052).  [XORIndex Loader](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1248) has been delivered to victims through code repository sites utilizing typo squatting naming conventions of various npm packages.(Citation: Socket BeaverTail XORIndex HexEval Contagious Interview July 2025)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1614 System Location Discovery\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1059.007 JavaScript\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1035": "# S1035 Small Sieve\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1035\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1035\n\n[Small Sieve](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1035) is a Telegram Bot API-based Python backdoor that has been distributed using a Nullsoft Scriptable Install System (NSIS) Installer; it has been used by [MuddyWater](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0069) since at least January 2022.(Citation: DHS CISA AA22-055A MuddyWater February 2022)(Citation: NCSC GCHQ Small Sieve Jan 2022)\n\nSecurity researchers have also noted [Small Sieve](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1035)'s use by UNC3313, which may be associated with [MuddyWater](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0069).(Citation: Mandiant UNC3313 Feb 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1132.002 Non-Standard Encoding\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1059.006 Python\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1102.002 Bidirectional Communication\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0143": "# S0143 Flame\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0143\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0143\n\n[Flame](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0143) is a sophisticated toolkit that has been used to collect information since at least 2010, largely targeting Middle East countries. (Citation: Kaspersky Flame)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1091 Replication Through Removable Media\n- T1210 Exploitation of Remote Services\n- T1123 Audio Capture\n- T1011.001 Exfiltration Over Bluetooth\n- T1036.010 Masquerade Account Name\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1136.001 Local Account\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1547.002 Authentication Package\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0698": "# S0698 HermeticWizard\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0698\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0698\n\n[HermeticWizard](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0698) is a worm that has been used to spread [HermeticWiper](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0697) in attacks against organizations in Ukraine since at least 2022.(Citation: ESET Hermetic Wizard March 2022)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1553.002 Code Signing\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1110.001 Password Guessing\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n- T1570 Lateral Tool Transfer\n- T1218.010 Regsvr32\n- T1559.001 Component Object Model\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1218.011 Rundll32\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0039": "# S0039 Net\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0039\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039\n\nThe [Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) utility is a component of the Windows operating system. It is used in command-line operations for control of users, groups, services, and network connections. (Citation: Microsoft Net Utility)\n\n[Net](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0039) has a great deal of functionality, (Citation: Savill 1999) much of which is useful for an adversary, such as gathering system and network information for Discovery, moving laterally through [SMB/Windows Admin Shares](https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1021/002) using <code>net use</code> commands, and interacting with services. The net1.exe utility is executed for certain functionality when net.exe is run and can be used directly in commands such as <code>net1 user</code>.\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1201 Password Policy Discovery\n- T1069.002 Domain Groups\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1087.002 Domain Account\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1007 System Service Discovery\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1070.005 Network Share Connection Removal\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1136.001 Local Account\n- T1098.007 Additional Local or Domain Groups\n- T1069.001 Local Groups\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1136.002 Domain Account\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0592": "# S0592 RemoteUtilities\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0592\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0592\n\n[RemoteUtilities](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0592) is a legitimate remote administration tool that has been used by [MuddyWater](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0069) since at least 2021 for execution on target machines.(Citation: Trend Micro Muddy Water March 2021)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1218.007 Msiexec\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1155": "# S1155 Covenant\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1155\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1155\n\n[Covenant](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1155) is a multi-platform command and control framework written in .NET. While designed for penetration testing and security research, the tool has also been used by threat actors such as [HAFNIUM](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0125) during operations. [Covenant](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1155) functions through a central listener managing multiple deployed \"Grunts\" that communicate back to the controller.(Citation: Github Covenant)(Citation: Microsoft HAFNIUM March 2020)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1571 Non-Standard Port\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1218.010 Regsvr32\n- T1218.004 InstallUtil\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1218.005 Mshta\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n", "mitre-attack-software-s1131": "# S1131 NPPSPY\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S1131\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S1131\n\nNPPSPY is an implementation of a theoretical mechanism first presented in 2004 for capturing credentials submitted to a Windows system via a rogue Network Provider API item. NPPSPY captures credentials following submission and writes them to a file on the victim system for follow-on exfiltration.(Citation: Huntress NPPSPY 2022)(Citation: Polak NPPSPY 2004)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1684.001 Impersonation\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1557 Adversary-in-the-Middle\n- T1552 Unsecured Credentials\n- T1056 Input Capture\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0521": "# S0521 BloodHound\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0521\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0521\n\n[BloodHound](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0521) is an Active Directory (AD) reconnaissance tool that can reveal hidden relationships and identify attack paths within an AD environment.(Citation: GitHub Bloodhound)(Citation: CrowdStrike BloodHound April 2018)(Citation: FoxIT Wocao December 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1069.002 Domain Groups\n- T1615 Group Policy Discovery\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1069.001 Local Groups\n- T1087.002 Domain Account\n- T1087.001 Local Account\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1482 Domain Trust Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9002": "# S9002 Diskpart\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9002\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9002\n\n[Diskpart](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9002) is a Windows command-line utility that is used to manage the computer’s drives, which includes disks, partitions, volumes and virtual hard disks.(Citation: Microsoft_diskpart_Feb2023)  \n\nAdversaries may abuse [Diskpart](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9002) to perform discovery and destructive actions on a system’s storage. For example, adversaries have been observed using [Diskpart](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9002) to conduct [Discovery](https://attack.mitre.org/tactics/TA0007) techniques to enumerate disks and volumes to gather information about the host environment, and to execute commands such as `clean all` to remove partition information and overwrite data across disks, resulting in data destruction.(Citation: Trendmicro_RansomHub_Dec2024)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1561.002 Disk Structure Wipe\n- T1222.001 Windows Permissions\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0160": "# S0160 certutil\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0160\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0160\n\n[certutil](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0160) is a command-line utility that can be used to obtain certificate authority information and configure Certificate Services. (Citation: TechNet Certutil)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1553.004 Install Root Certificate\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0110": "# S0110 at\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0110\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0110\n\n[at](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0110) is used to schedule tasks on a system to run at a specified date or time.(Citation: TechNet At)(Citation: Linux at)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1053.002 At\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0116": "# S0116 UACMe\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0116\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0116\n\n[UACMe](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0116) is an open source assessment tool that contains many methods for bypassing Windows User Account Control on multiple versions of the operating system. (Citation: Github UACMe)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0445": "# S0445 ShimRatReporter\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0445\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0445\n\n[ShimRatReporter](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0445) is a tool used by suspected Chinese adversary [Mofang](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0103) to automatically conduct initial discovery. The details from this discovery are used to customize follow-on payloads (such as [ShimRat](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0444)) as well as set up faux infrastructure which mimics the adversary's targets. [ShimRatReporter](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0445) has been used in campaigns targeting multiple countries and sectors including government, military, critical infrastructure, automobile, and weapons development.(Citation: FOX-IT May 2016 Mofang)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1518 Software Discovery\n- T1087 Account Discovery\n- T1560 Archive Collected Data\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1069 Permission Groups Discovery\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1020 Automated Exfiltration\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0633": "# S0633 Sliver\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0633\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0633\n\n[Sliver](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0633) is an open source, cross-platform, red team command and control (C2) framework written in Golang. [Sliver](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0633) includes its own package manager, \"armory,\" for staging and downloading additional tools and payloads to the primary C2 framework.(Citation: Bishop Fox Sliver Framework August 2019)(Citation: Cybereason Sliver Undated)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1027.004 Compile After Delivery\n- T1001.002 Steganography\n- T1134 Access Token Manipulation\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1090.001 Internal Proxy\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1113 Screen Capture\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1573.001 Symmetric Cryptography\n- T1071 Application Layer Protocol\n- T1558.001 Golden Ticket\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1132.001 Standard Encoding\n- T1003.001 LSASS Memory\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s0692": "# S0692 SILENTTRINITY\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S0692\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0692\n\n[SILENTTRINITY](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0692) is an open source remote administration and post-exploitation framework primarily written in Python that includes stagers written in Powershell, C, and Boo. [SILENTTRINITY](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0692) was used in a 2019 campaign against Croatian government agencies by unidentified cyber actors.(Citation: GitHub SILENTTRINITY March 2022)(Citation: Security Affairs SILENTTRINITY July 2019)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1552.006 Group Policy Preferences\n- T1620 Reflective Code Loading\n- T1069.002 Domain Groups\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1546.001 Change Default File Association