tre.org/software/S9051) has been affiliated with the threat group [The Gentlemen](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1058).(Citation: MSTI_GentelmenRansomware_May2026)(Citation: TrendAI_TheGentlemen_Sept2025)(Citation: CarveyODonnellWelchHuntress_TheGentlemen_May2026)(Citation: CheckPointResearch_TheGentlemen_April2026)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1070.003 Clear Command History\n- T1059.012 Hypervisor CLI\n- T1222.001 Windows Permissions\n- T1027.010 Command Obfuscation\n- T1053.003 Cron\n- T1678 Delay Execution\n- T1070.007 Clear Network Connection History and Configurations\n- T1037.003 Network Logon Script\n- T1053.005 Scheduled Task\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1529 System Shutdown/Reboot\n- T1689 Downgrade Attack\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1484.001 Group Policy Modification\n- T1036.004 Masquerade Task or Service\n- T1564.012 File/Path Exclusions\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1037.004 RC Scripts\n- T1675 ESXi Administration Command\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1482 Domain Trust Discovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1690 Prevent Command History Logging\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1547.001 Registry Run Keys / Startup Folder\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1491.001 Internal Defacement\n- T1685.005 Clear Windows Event Logs\n- T1561.001 Disk Content Wipe\n- T1490 Inhibit System Recovery\n- T1090 Proxy\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1680 Local Storage Discovery\n- T1686.003 Windows Host Firewall\n- T1679 Selective Exclusion\n- T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1021.001 Remote Desktop Protocol\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1047 Windows Management Instrumentation\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9045": "# S9045 BACKUPOSINT v9.0 APEX PREDATOR\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9045\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9045\n\n[BACKUPOSINT v9.0 APEX PREDATOR](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9045) is a 49-module Python malware developed entirely by Anthropic’s Claude Code as part of the [AI-Assisted Breach of Mexico's Government Infrastructure](https://attack.mitre.org/campaigns/C0064) campaign. [BACKUPOSINT v9.0 APEX PREDATOR](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9045) leverages publicly available techniques for network enumeration, credential harvesting, Active Directory interrogation, database access, privilege escalation, cloud metadata extraction, and lateral movement (Citation: AI-Mexico-Govt-Compromise).\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1046 Network Service Discovery\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n- T1552.005 Cloud Instance Metadata API\n- T1555.006 Cloud Secrets Management Stores\n- T1053.003 Cron\n- T1018 Remote System Discovery\n- T1567 Exfiltration Over Web Service\n- T1683 Generate Content\n- T1135 Network Share Discovery\n- T1090.002 External Proxy\n- T1059.009 Cloud API\n- T1552.003 Shell History\n- T1548.003 Sudo and Sudo Caching\n- T1552.001 Credentials In Files\n- T1528 Steal Application Access Token\n- T1609 Container Administration Command\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9050": "# S9050 Vidar\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9050\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9050\n\n[Vidar](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9050) is a Windows infostealer that was first advertised in November 2018. Written in C++, [Vidar](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9050) collects PII, cryptocurrency wallets, documents and other data within the compromised machine. Version 2 of [Vidar](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9050) was published in October 2025, and has been used in numerous operations since its release. [Vidar](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9050) has been observed alongside other malware strains, including STOP/Djvu ransomware, Mina.exe (an XMRIG miner), and Clip1.exe (a Laplas Clipper sample).(Citation: Intrinsec_Vidar_April2026)(Citation: LevelBlue_Vidar_May2026)(Citation: CyberAus_Vidar_May2026)(Citation: Unit42_Vidar_July2026)(Citation: Cyfirma_Vidar_June2024)(Citation: Logpoint_StealerMalwareFamilies_2024)(Citation: CISA Scattered Spider Advisory November 2023)(Citation: QuorumCyber_Vidar_March2023)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1055.012 Process Hollowing\n- T1480 Execution Guardrails\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1204.004 Malicious Copy and Paste\n- T1059.001 PowerShell\n- T1569.002 Service Execution\n- T1539 Steal Web Session Cookie\n- T1566.002 Spearphishing Link\n- T1622 Debugger Evasion\n- T1071.004 DNS\n- T1678 Delay Execution\n- T1027.001 Binary Padding\n- T1489 Service Stop\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1119 Automated Collection\n- T1041 Exfiltration Over C2 Channel\n- T1614 System Location Discovery\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1071.001 Web Protocols\n- T1497.003 Time Based Checks\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1055.003 Thread Execution Hijacking\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1552.001 Credentials In Files\n- T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1005 Data from Local System\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1204.001 Malicious Link\n- T1555.003 Credentials from Web Browsers\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1102.001 Dead Drop Resolver\n- T1573.002 Asymmetric Cryptography\n- T1497.001 System Checks\n- T1222.001 Windows Permissions\n- T1685 Disable or Modify Tools\n- T1564.001 Hidden Files and Directories\n- T1560.001 Archive via Utility\n- T1027 Obfuscated Files or Information\n- T1106 Native API\n- T1217 Browser Information Discovery\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1036.001 Invalid Code Signature\n- T1129 Shared Modules\n- T1027.005 Indicator Removal from Tools\n- T1543 Create or Modify System Process\n- T1189 Drive-by Compromise\n- T1016 System Network Configuration Discovery\n- T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer\n- T1574.001 DLL\n- T1057 Process Discovery\n", "mitre-attack-software-s9046": "# S9046 IOControl\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** S9046\n**Type:** software\n**Aliases:** n/a\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9046\n\n[IOControl](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9046) is a Linux backdoor compiled for 32-bit ARM devices that has been used to target IoT and OT devices in Israel and the United States, including IP cameras, routers, firewalls, PLCs, HMIs, and Orpak and Gasboy fuel management systems. Affected vendors include Baicells, D-Link, Hikvision, Phoenix Contact, Red Lion, Teltonika, and Unitronics. [IOControl](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S9046) has been used by [CyberAv3ngers](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G1027) which is suspected to be affiliated with the Iranian Government Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC).(Citation: Claroty-IOCONTROL)(Citation: Dragos 2025 Year in Review)(Citation: Armis-IOCONTROL)\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1027.002 Software Packing\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1027.013 Encrypted/Encoded File\n- T1070.009 Clear Persistence\n- T1082 System Information Discovery\n- T1124 System Time Discovery\n- T1037.004 RC Scripts\n", "mitre-attack-group-g0082": "# G0082 APT38\n\n**ATT&CK ID:** G0082\n**Type:** group\n**Aliases:** APT38, NICKEL GLADSTONE, BeagleBoyz, Bluenoroff, Stardust Chollima, Sapphire Sleet, COPERNICIUM\n**Reference:** https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0082\n\n[APT38](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0082) is a North Korean state-sponsored threat group that specializes in financial cyber operations; it has been attributed to the Reconnaissance General Bureau.(Citation: CISA AA20-239A BeagleBoyz August 2020) Active since at least 2014, [APT38](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0082) has targeted banks, financial institutions, casinos, cryptocurrency exchanges, SWIFT system endpoints, and ATMs in at least 38 countries worldwide. Significant operations include the 2016 Bank of Bangladesh heist, Bancomext (Citation: FireEye APT38 Oct 2018) and Banco de Chile (Citation: FireEye APT38 Oct 2018), along with software supply chain compromises and destructive malware attacks.(Citation: CISA AA20-239A BeagleBoyz August 2020)(Citation: FireEye APT38 Oct 2018)(Citation: DOJ North Korea Indictment Feb 2021)(Citation: Kaspersky Lazarus Under The Hood Blog 2017)(Citation: Microsoft Axios Compromise APR 2026)(Citation: Microsoft Sapphire Sleet APR 2026)(Citation: MIcrosoft Mastra npm Compromise JUN 2026)\n\nNorth Korean group definitions are known to have significant overlap, and some security researchers report all North Korean state-sponsored cyber activity under the name [Lazarus Group](https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0032) instead of tracking clusters or subgroups.\n\n## Techniques used\n- T1486 Data Encrypted for Impact\n- T1055 Process Injection\n- T1033 System Owner/User Discovery\n- T1657 Financial Theft\n- T1112 Modify Registry\n- T1036.005 Match Legitimate Resource Name or Location\n- T1677 Poisoned Pipeline Execution\n- T1049 System Network Connections Discovery\n- T1070.004 File Deletion\n- T1036.006 Space after Filename\n- T1608.001 Upload Malware\n- T1056.001 Keylogging\n- T1056.002 GUI Input Capture\n- T1518.001 Security Software Discovery\n- T1543.003 Windows Service\n- T1564.011 Ignore Process Interrupts\n- T1213.006 Databases\n- T1548.002 Bypass User Account Control\n- T1678 Delay Execution\n- T1189 Drive-by Compromise\n- T1683.002 Audio-Visual Content\n- T1053.006 Systemd Timers\n- T1083 File and Directory Discovery\n- T1213.005 Messaging Applications\n- T1070.003 Clear Command History\n- T1059.003 Windows Command Shell\n- T1140 Deobfuscate/Decode Files or Information\n- T1059.002 AppleScript\n- T1685.003 Modify or Spoof Tool UI\n- T1585.002 Email Accounts\n- T1059.005 Visual Basic\n- T1074.001 Local Data Staging\n- T1585.001 Social Media Account